window.QBANK=[{"id": "0-1", "ch": 0, "n": 1, "stem": "Which antibiotic is active <strong>only</strong> against gram-positive bacteria?", "opts": ["Gentamicin", "Vancomycin", "Meropenem", "Ciprofloxacin"], "a": 1, "sec": ["2.2"]}, {"id": "0-2", "ch": 0, "n": 2, "stem": "Which antibiotic is active against <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>?", "opts": ["Ceftriaxone", "Amoxicillin", "Clindamycin", "Piperacillin-tazobactam"], "a": 3, "sec": ["2.2", "2.3"]}, {"id": "0-3", "ch": 0, "n": 3, "stem": "Daptomycin should not be used to treat pneumonia because:", "opts": ["It is inactivated by pulmonary surfactant", "It does not cover MRSA", "It is not available intravenously", "It is only bacteriostatic"], "a": 0, "sec": ["2.2", "3.2"]}, {"id": "0-4", "ch": 0, "n": 4, "stem": "The PK/PD index that best predicts the efficacy of β-lactams is:", "opts": ["Cmax/MIC", "AUC<sub>24</sub>/MIC", "Percentage of the dosing interval with free concentration above the MIC (%fT&gt;MIC)", "Duration of the post-antibiotic effect"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "0-5", "ch": 0, "n": 5, "stem": "Once-daily dosing of aminoglycosides is justified mainly because:", "opts": ["Aminoglycosides are time-dependent antibiotics with no post-antibiotic effect", "Aminoglycosides are well absorbed orally", "Killing is concentration-dependent and the post-antibiotic effect is long, while toxicity is lower with a long dosing interval", "Aminoglycosides accumulate in the CSF"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.1", "6.2"]}, {"id": "0-6", "ch": 0, "n": 6, "stem": "Therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM) is most useful for antibiotics that:", "opts": ["Have a narrow therapeutic window, such as vancomycin and aminoglycosides", "Are well absorbed orally", "Are eliminated mainly in the bile", "Have a wide safety margin, such as penicillins"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.5"]}, {"id": "0-7", "ch": 0, "n": 7, "stem": "A 25-year-old trauma patient with sepsis in the ICU has a creatinine clearance of 180 mL/min. The most likely consequence for β-lactam therapy at standard doses is:", "opts": ["Accumulation and neurotoxicity", "Sub-therapeutic concentrations because of augmented renal clearance", "No change in exposure", "Increased protein binding"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "0-8", "ch": 0, "n": 8, "stem": "Carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales (CRE) are a major concern mainly because:", "opts": ["They are resistant only to penicillins", "They are reliably treated with ceftriaxone", "They occur only in the community", "They are resistant to almost all β-lactams, leaving few treatment options"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "0-9", "ch": 0, "n": 9, "stem": "A severe bloodstream infection caused by ESBL-producing <em>E. coli</em> is usually treated with:", "opts": ["Ceftriaxone", "Amoxicillin", "Ampicillin", "A carbapenem"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.2", "5.4"]}, {"id": "0-10", "ch": 0, "n": 10, "stem": "The strongest single risk factor for infection by a given multidrug-resistant organism is:", "opts": ["Age over 65 years", "Previous colonization or infection with that organism", "Diabetes", "Female sex"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "0-11", "ch": 0, "n": 11, "stem": "De-escalation of antibiotic therapy means:", "opts": ["Narrowing the spectrum or reducing the number of antibiotics once culture results are known", "Increasing the dose of the current antibiotic", "Adding a second antibiotic", "Switching from oral to intravenous therapy"], "a": 0, "sec": ["7.2"]}, {"id": "0-12", "ch": 0, "n": 12, "stem": "A multidrug-resistant (MDR) organism is defined as non-susceptible to:", "opts": ["A single antibiotic", "Penicillin only", "At least one agent in three or more antimicrobial categories", "All available antibiotics"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "0-13", "ch": 0, "n": 13, "stem": "According to current sepsis guidelines, in a patient with septic shock antibiotics should be given:", "opts": ["Immediately, ideally within 1 hour of recognition", "Within 3 hours, after imaging", "After the result of procalcitonin", "Only after blood culture results are available"], "a": 0, "sec": ["4.1"]}, {"id": "0-14", "ch": 0, "n": 14, "stem": "Which of the following is <strong>not</strong> an accepted rationale for combination antibiotic therapy?", "opts": ["Synergy in <em>Enterococcus faecalis</em> endocarditis", "Toxin suppression with clindamycin in necrotizing fasciitis", "Prevention of resistance in tuberculosis", "Routine definitive treatment of bacteremia by a fully susceptible <em>E. coli</em>"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.4"]}, {"id": "0-15", "ch": 0, "n": 15, "stem": "For many common infections (e.g. community-acquired pneumonia, uncomplicated gram-negative bacteremia), short antibiotic courses compared with longer ones:", "opts": ["Are less effective", "Are as effective, with fewer adverse effects", "Increase antimicrobial resistance", "Are never appropriate"], "a": 1, "sec": ["7.4"]}, {"id": "0-16", "ch": 0, "n": 16, "stem": "Which antibiotic has excellent oral bioavailability, allowing an early IV-to-oral switch with similar blood levels?", "opts": ["Vancomycin", "Ceftriaxone", "Linezolid", "Gentamicin"], "a": 2, "sec": ["3.3", "7.3"]}, {"id": "0-17", "ch": 0, "n": 17, "stem": "In the AWaRe classification, which antibiotic belongs to the <strong>Reserve</strong> group?", "opts": ["Amoxicillin", "Ciprofloxacin", "Colistin", "Ceftriaxone"], "a": 2, "sec": ["2.6"]}, {"id": "0-18", "ch": 0, "n": 18, "stem": "A patient reports a mild, non-urticarial rash with amoxicillin 20 years ago and now needs treatment for pyelonephritis. The most appropriate statement is:", "opts": ["A β-lactam with a dissimilar side chain can usually be given; the allergy label should be evaluated", "All β-lactams are absolutely contraindicated", "Carbapenems carry a 50% risk of cross-reactivity", "Only vancomycin can be used"], "a": 0, "sec": ["4.2"]}, {"id": "0-19", "ch": 0, "n": 19, "stem": "Which antibiotic is appropriate for an intracellular pathogen such as <em>Legionella pneumophila</em>?", "opts": ["Ceftriaxone", "Amoxicillin", "Vancomycin", "Levofloxacin"], "a": 3, "sec": ["2.3", "3.2"]}, {"id": "0-20", "ch": 0, "n": 20, "stem": "In an antibiogram reported with current European breakpoints, the category \"I\" means:", "opts": ["Intermediate: the drug should be avoided", "Susceptible, increased exposure", "Intrinsic resistance", "Insufficient data to interpret"], "a": 1, "sec": ["2.5"]}, {"id": "1-1", "ch": 1, "n": 1, "stem": "According to Sepsis-3, sepsis is defined as:", "opts": ["Infection plus at least 2 SIRS criteria", "Life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection", "Bacteremia with a positive blood culture", "Infection with hypotension unresponsive to fluids"], "a": 1, "sec": ["2.1", "5.1"]}, {"id": "1-2", "ch": 1, "n": 2, "stem": "Which combination identifies septic shock in an adult according to Sepsis-3?", "opts": ["SBP &lt;90 mmHg and heart rate &gt;90/min", "qSOFA ≥2 and fever", "Lactate &gt;4 mmol/L, regardless of blood pressure", "Vasopressors needed to keep MAP ≥65 mmHg and lactate &gt;2 mmol/L despite adequate fluid resuscitation"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "1-3", "ch": 1, "n": 3, "stem": "Which of the following are the three qSOFA criteria?", "opts": ["Respiratory rate ≥22/min, altered mentation, systolic BP ≤100 mmHg", "Temperature &gt;38 °C, heart rate &gt;90/min, WBC &gt;12,000/µL", "Lactate &gt;2 mmol/L, oliguria, platelet count &lt;100,000/µL", "PaO₂/FiO₂ &lt;300, creatinine &gt;2 mg/dL, bilirubin &gt;2 mg/dL"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "1-4", "ch": 1, "n": 4, "stem": "For screening hospitalized adults for sepsis, current guidelines recommend:", "opts": ["qSOFA as the single preferred tool", "Procalcitonin measurement in all febrile patients", "NEWS, NEWS2, MEWS or SIRS over qSOFA as a single screening tool", "No screening, only clinical judgement"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "1-5", "ch": 1, "n": 5, "stem": "The most common source of sepsis in adults is:", "opts": ["The urinary tract", "The central nervous system", "The lungs", "The skin"], "a": 2, "sec": ["1.1"]}, {"id": "1-6", "ch": 1, "n": 6, "stem": "A 72-year-old woman presents with fever, flank pain, confusion and hypotension. Urinalysis shows leukocytes and nitrites. The most likely pathogen is:", "opts": ["<em>Escherichia coli</em>", "<em>Streptococcus pyogenes</em>", "<em>Neisseria meningitidis</em>", "<em>Candida albicans</em>"], "a": 0, "sec": ["1.1"]}, {"id": "1-7", "ch": 1, "n": 7, "stem": "Which statement about hyperlactatemia in sepsis is correct?", "opts": ["It is always caused by tissue hypoxia", "It is never seen in patients with normal blood pressure", "It has no prognostic value", "It can also result from β2-adrenergic stimulation of aerobic glycolysis and reduced hepatic clearance"], "a": 3, "sec": ["3.5"]}, {"id": "1-8", "ch": 1, "n": 8, "stem": "Which finding is typical of sepsis-induced immunosuppression?", "opts": ["Increased monocyte HLA-DR expression", "Lymphocyte apoptosis and reduced monocyte HLA-DR expression", "Increased number of circulating dendritic cells", "Absence of anti-inflammatory cytokines"], "a": 1, "sec": ["3.2"]}, {"id": "1-9", "ch": 1, "n": 9, "stem": "A patient with pneumonia is hypotensive and requires norepinephrine. According to current guidelines, antimicrobials should be given:", "opts": ["Immediately, ideally within 1 hour of recognition", "Within 3 hours, after a full diagnostic work-up", "Only after the blood culture becomes positive", "Within 6 hours, after procalcitonin is available"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "1-10", "ch": 1, "n": 10, "stem": "A patient with fever and borderline tachycardia, no hypotension and no clear source has possible sepsis. Current guidelines suggest:", "opts": ["Antimicrobials immediately, before any assessment", "No antimicrobials for at least 24 hours", "A rapid time-limited assessment, then antimicrobials within 3 hours if concern for infection persists", "Starting antimicrobials only if procalcitonin is high"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "1-11", "ch": 1, "n": 11, "stem": "Which statement about blood cultures in sepsis is correct?", "opts": ["A single set is enough", "They should be collected only after the first dose of antibiotics", "They are not needed if the source is obvious", "At least 2 sets should be collected before antimicrobials, without delaying antimicrobial therapy"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "1-12", "ch": 1, "n": 12, "stem": "The first-line vasopressor in adult septic shock is:", "opts": ["Dopamine", "Norepinephrine", "Vasopressin", "Phenylephrine"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.6"]}, {"id": "1-13", "ch": 1, "n": 13, "stem": "The recommended initial mean arterial pressure target in adult septic shock is:", "opts": ["55 mmHg", "65 mmHg", "80–85 mmHg", "100 mmHg"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.6"]}, {"id": "1-14", "ch": 1, "n": 14, "stem": "Which fluid is preferred for the initial resuscitation of a patient with septic shock and no brain injury?", "opts": ["Hydroxyethyl starch", "20% albumin alone", "Balanced crystalloid (e.g. Ringer's lactate)", "5% dextrose"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.5"]}, {"id": "1-15", "ch": 1, "n": 15, "stem": "In sepsis-induced hypoperfusion, current guidelines suggest an initial crystalloid volume of:", "opts": ["At least 30 mL/kg within the first 3 hours", "5 mL/kg within 24 hours", "100 mL/kg within the first hour", "No fluids until a central venous catheter is placed"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.5"]}, {"id": "1-16", "ch": 1, "n": 16, "stem": "Which statement about intravenous vitamin C in sepsis is correct?", "opts": ["It is recommended in all patients with septic shock", "It is the first-line treatment of lactic acidosis", "It halves mortality in septic shock", "Guidelines suggest against it, because randomized evidence showed no benefit and possible harm"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.8"]}, {"id": "1-17", "ch": 1, "n": 17, "stem": "A patient with septic shock has an obstructed, infected kidney (stone with hydronephrosis). The best approach is:", "opts": ["Antibiotics alone for 14 days, then elective urology referral", "Wait for blood culture results before any intervention", "Corticosteroids instead of antibiotics", "Antibiotics plus urgent decompression, ideally within 6 hours of diagnosis"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.4"]}, {"id": "1-18", "ch": 1, "n": 18, "stem": "In clinically stable hospitalized patients with uncomplicated bloodstream infection (no severe immunosuppression and no focus needing prolonged therapy):", "opts": ["7 days of antibiotics are as effective as 14 days", "14 days of antibiotics reduce mortality compared with 7 days", "3 days of antibiotics are as effective as 14 days", "Only 6 weeks of intravenous therapy are effective"], "a": 0, "sec": ["7.1"]}, {"id": "1-19", "ch": 1, "n": 19, "stem": "According to current pediatric sepsis criteria, septic shock in a child is defined as:", "opts": ["Any child with fever and tachycardia", "SIRS plus hypotension", "Sepsis (Phoenix Sepsis Score ≥2) with at least 1 cardiovascular point", "A positive blood culture and lactate &gt;2 mmol/L"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.4"]}, {"id": "1-20", "ch": 1, "n": 20, "stem": "Which statement about the long-term course after sepsis is correct?", "opts": ["Survivors return to their previous health within a month", "Survivors are at risk of physical, cognitive and psychological impairment and of rehospitalization, often for infection", "Follow-up is not needed once antibiotics are stopped", "Cognitive impairment after sepsis is always reversible within days"], "a": 1, "sec": ["7.2"]}, {"id": "2-1", "ch": 2, "n": 1, "stem": "In a large cohort of adults with community-acquired bacterial meningitis, the classic triad of fever, neck stiffness and altered mental status was present in approximately:", "opts": ["95% of patients", "44% of patients", "10% of patients", "80% of patients"], "a": 1, "sec": ["4.1"]}, {"id": "2-2", "ch": 2, "n": 2, "stem": "The most common cause of community-acquired bacterial meningitis in adults in Europe is:", "opts": ["<em>Neisseria meningitidis</em>", "<em>Listeria monocytogenes</em>", "<em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> type b", "<em>Streptococcus pneumoniae</em>"], "a": 3, "sec": ["1.1", "2"]}, {"id": "2-3", "ch": 2, "n": 3, "stem": "A 68-year-old man presents with fever, headache and confusion. The most appropriate empiric regimen, in a setting with pneumococcal cephalosporin resistance, is:", "opts": ["Ceftriaxone + vancomycin + amoxicillin", "Ceftriaxone alone", "Ceftriaxone + azithromycin", "Vancomycin + ciprofloxacin"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "2-4", "ch": 2, "n": 4, "stem": "Amoxicillin/ampicillin is added to empiric therapy in patients over 50 because:", "opts": ["It covers penicillin-resistant pneumococci", "It covers methicillin-resistant <em>S. aureus</em>", "<em>Listeria monocytogenes</em> is intrinsically resistant to cephalosporins", "It covers anaerobes from the sinuses"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "2-5", "ch": 2, "n": 5, "stem": "According to current guidelines, which finding is an indication for brain imaging before lumbar puncture?", "opts": ["Age over 60 years alone", "Fever with neck stiffness", "An isolated sixth cranial nerve palsy", "New-onset seizures"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "2-6", "ch": 2, "n": 6, "stem": "A patient with suspected bacterial meningitis needs a CT before LP. The correct sequence is:", "opts": ["CT, then LP, then antibiotics", "Blood cultures, dexamethasone and antibiotics, then CT, then LP", "LP first, then antibiotics after the Gram stain", "Oral antibiotics while awaiting CT"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "2-7", "ch": 2, "n": 7, "stem": "CSF shows 2,500 white cells/µL (90% neutrophils), CSF:blood glucose ratio 0.2 and protein 3 g/L. The most likely diagnosis is:", "opts": ["Viral meningitis", "Normal CSF", "Bacterial meningitis", "Cryptococcal meningitis"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "2-8", "ch": 2, "n": 8, "stem": "A patient has a 3-week history of headache and fever, a sixth nerve palsy, hyponatremia, lymphocytic CSF with very low glucose and protein 2.5 g/L, and basal meningeal enhancement with hydrocephalus on MRI. The most likely diagnosis is:", "opts": ["Tuberculous meningitis", "Enteroviral meningitis", "Meningococcal meningitis", "HSV-2 meningitis"], "a": 0, "sec": ["4.4", "5.3", "5.4"]}, {"id": "2-9", "ch": 2, "n": 9, "stem": "Regarding adjunctive dexamethasone in adult bacterial meningitis:", "opts": ["It should be started only after a positive CSF culture", "It should be started before or with the first antibiotic dose, for 4 days, with the greatest benefit in pneumococcal meningitis", "It is recommended for neonates", "It is recommended for cryptococcal meningitis"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "2-10", "ch": 2, "n": 10, "stem": "A patient with advanced HIV infection (CD4 40/µL) has had a slowly worsening headache for 3 weeks, with mild neck stiffness. Which test on CSF and serum best confirms the most likely diagnosis?", "opts": ["Gram stain and bacterial culture", "Enterovirus PCR", "Cryptococcal antigen (CrAg)", "Antistreptolysin O titer"], "a": 2, "sec": ["1.2", "5.4"]}, {"id": "2-11", "ch": 2, "n": 11, "stem": "The recommended treatment duration for uncomplicated meningococcal meningitis is:", "opts": ["21 days", "14 days", "3 days", "7 days"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "2-12", "ch": 2, "n": 12, "stem": "The recommended treatment duration for <em>Listeria</em> meningitis is:", "opts": ["At least 21 days", "5 days", "7 days", "10 days"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "2-13", "ch": 2, "n": 13, "stem": "Which drug is used for chemoprophylaxis of close contacts of a patient with meningococcal meningitis?", "opts": ["Amoxicillin", "Doxycycline", "Ciprofloxacin", "Vancomycin"], "a": 2, "sec": ["8.2"]}, {"id": "2-14", "ch": 2, "n": 14, "stem": "A man has his third episode of pneumococcal meningitis, two years after a head injury. The most likely predisposing condition is:", "opts": ["A CSF leak from a basilar skull fracture", "A terminal complement (C5–C9) deficiency", "Recurrent HSV-2 infection", "Toscana virus infection"], "a": 0, "sec": ["3", "7.2"]}, {"id": "2-15", "ch": 2, "n": 15, "stem": "Recurrent invasive meningococcal disease is classically associated with:", "opts": ["Selective IgA deficiency", "Chronic granulomatous disease", "DiGeorge syndrome", "Terminal complement deficiency or complement-inhibitor therapy"], "a": 3, "sec": ["2", "7.2"]}, {"id": "2-16", "ch": 2, "n": 16, "stem": "The most common cause of viral meningitis is:", "opts": ["HSV-1", "Enteroviruses", "Cytomegalovirus", "Epstein–Barr virus"], "a": 1, "sec": ["1.2"]}, {"id": "2-17", "ch": 2, "n": 17, "stem": "In summer, a patient from central Italy develops lymphocytic meningitis after sandfly bites. The most likely cause is:", "opts": ["Toscana virus", "West Nile virus", "Tick-borne encephalitis virus", "Lymphocytic choriomeningitis virus"], "a": 0, "sec": ["1.2"]}, {"id": "2-18", "ch": 2, "n": 18, "stem": "After pneumococcal meningitis, which follow-up step is the most important?", "opts": ["A routine end-of-treatment lumbar puncture", "A routine brain MRI at 6 months", "An early hearing assessment, ideally before discharge", "An EEG in every patient"], "a": 2, "sec": ["7.2"]}, {"id": "2-19", "ch": 2, "n": 19, "stem": "A repeat lumbar puncture during treatment of bacterial meningitis is indicated when:", "opts": ["Every patient completes therapy", "The cause is meningococcus", "Never, because it carries no information", "There is no clinical improvement within 48 hours of appropriate therapy"], "a": 3, "sec": ["7.1"]}, {"id": "2-20", "ch": 2, "n": 20, "stem": "Which vaccine was developed for the African meningitis belt and covers serogroup X?", "opts": ["MenB (protein-based)", "Men5CV (A, C, W, Y, X)", "PCV20", "Hib conjugate"], "a": 1, "sec": ["8.1"]}, {"id": "3-1", "ch": 3, "n": 1, "stem": "In high-income countries, the most common cause of infective endocarditis is:", "opts": ["Oral viridans group streptococci", "<em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>", "<em>Enterococcus faecalis</em>", "Coagulase-negative staphylococci"], "a": 1, "sec": ["1"]}, {"id": "3-2", "ch": 3, "n": 2, "stem": "A 72-year-old man has IE caused by <em>Streptococcus gallolyticus</em>. Which further investigation is most important?", "opts": ["Upper limb venous Doppler", "Dental panoramic X-ray only", "Bone marrow biopsy", "Colonoscopy"], "a": 3, "sec": ["1"]}, {"id": "3-3", "ch": 3, "n": 3, "stem": "In people who inject drugs, the valve most typically involved in IE is the:", "opts": ["Tricuspid valve", "Mitral valve", "Aortic valve", "Pulmonary valve"], "a": 0, "sec": ["2.1"]}, {"id": "3-4", "ch": 3, "n": 4, "stem": "Osler nodes are best described as:", "opts": ["Painless haemorrhagic macules on the palms and soles", "Retinal haemorrhages with a pale centre", "Painful nodules on the pads of the fingers and toes, of immunological origin", "Linear dark streaks under the nails"], "a": 2, "sec": ["4"]}, {"id": "3-5", "ch": 3, "n": 5, "stem": "In suspected IE in a haemodynamically stable patient, blood cultures should be taken as:", "opts": ["At least three sets before starting antibiotics", "One set after the first antibiotic dose", "Only if the temperature exceeds 39 °C", "Only from an arterial line"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "3-6", "ch": 3, "n": 6, "stem": "According to the current Duke criteria, definite IE on clinical grounds requires:", "opts": ["1 major + 1 minor criterion", "2 major, or 1 major + 3 minor, or 5 minor criteria", "3 minor criteria", "1 major criterion alone"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.5"]}, {"id": "3-7", "ch": 3, "n": 7, "stem": "Which of the following is a major criterion in the current Duke criteria?", "opts": ["Fever &gt;38.0 °C", "Positive rheumatoid factor", "Splinter haemorrhages", "Evidence of IE at direct inspection during heart surgery"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.5"]}, {"id": "3-8", "ch": 3, "n": 8, "stem": "The first-line imaging test in suspected IE is:", "opts": ["Cardiac MRI", "FDG-PET/CT", "Transthoracic echocardiography", "Coronary angiography"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "3-9", "ch": 3, "n": 9, "stem": "A patient with a mechanical aortic valve has <em>S. aureus</em> bacteraemia; TOE is negative but suspicion remains high. The best next step is:", "opts": ["Rule out IE and stop work-up", "Stop antibiotics and repeat blood cultures in 1 month", "Repeat TOE in 5–7 days and/or perform cardiac CT or FDG-PET/CT", "Perform cardiac catheterisation"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "3-10", "ch": 3, "n": 10, "stem": "The most common cause of culture-negative IE is:", "opts": ["Antibiotic therapy given before blood cultures", "<em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>", "<em>Escherichia coli</em>", "<em>Candida albicans</em>"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.4"]}, {"id": "3-11", "ch": 3, "n": 11, "stem": "Which finding is a major microbiological criterion for <em>Coxiella burnetii</em> IE?", "opts": ["Phase II IgM ≥1:50", "A single positive blood culture", "Positive rheumatoid factor", "Phase I IgG titre &gt;1:800"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.4", "5.5"]}, {"id": "3-12", "ch": 3, "n": 12, "stem": "In native valve IE caused by <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>, which statement about therapy is correct?", "opts": ["Vancomycin is the first choice for both MSSA and MRSA", "MSSA is treated with an antistaphylococcal penicillin or cefazolin; MRSA with vancomycin or daptomycin", "Gentamicin should be added routinely for MSSA", "MRSA is treated with high-dose cefazolin"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "3-13", "ch": 3, "n": 13, "stem": "In IE with initially positive blood cultures, the duration of antibiotic therapy is counted from:", "opts": ["The day of hospital admission", "The first day of negative blood cultures on effective therapy", "The day of the first echocardiogram", "The day fever resolves"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "3-14", "ch": 3, "n": 14, "stem": "In stable patients with left-sided IE who respond to initial intravenous therapy:", "opts": ["Switching to oral antibiotics after at least 10 days of IV therapy is as effective as continuing IV therapy", "Oral therapy from day 1 is superior to IV therapy", "Two weeks of therapy are enough for all organisms", "Surgery is never needed"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.4"]}, {"id": "3-15", "ch": 3, "n": 15, "stem": "Which situation is an indication for emergency (within 24 hours) surgery?", "opts": ["A 6 mm mitral vegetation without complications", "Fever lasting 3 days after starting antibiotics", "Severe acute aortic regurgitation causing refractory pulmonary oedema", "Microscopic haematuria"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.5"]}, {"id": "3-16", "ch": 3, "n": 16, "stem": "A patient with a pacemaker has device-related IE. The correct management is:", "opts": ["Antibiotics alone for 2 weeks", "Removal of the generator only, leaving the leads", "Antifungal therapy", "Complete removal of the device and all leads, plus antibiotics"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.5"]}, {"id": "3-17", "ch": 3, "n": 17, "stem": "According to current guidelines, which patient is at high risk and should receive antibiotic prophylaxis before a high-risk dental procedure?", "opts": ["A patient with previous IE", "A patient with an isolated atrial septal defect never repaired", "A patient with coronary artery bypass grafts", "A patient with hypertension"], "a": 0, "sec": ["8.2"]}, {"id": "3-18", "ch": 3, "n": 18, "stem": "In a high-risk patient, antibiotic prophylaxis is recommended before:", "opts": ["Bronchoscopy without biopsy", "Colonoscopy", "Dental extraction", "Local anaesthetic injection into non-infected tissue"], "a": 2, "sec": ["8.2"]}, {"id": "3-19", "ch": 3, "n": 19, "stem": "Which of the following antibiotics is not an option for IE prophylaxis in penicillin-allergic patients?", "opts": ["Azithromycin", "Clarithromycin", "Doxycycline", "Piperacillin/tazobactam"], "a": 3, "sec": ["8.2"]}, {"id": "3-20", "ch": 3, "n": 20, "stem": "In native valve IE caused by methicillin-susceptible <em>S. aureus</em>, adding gentamicin is:", "opts": ["Mandatory for 6 weeks", "Not recommended, because of nephrotoxicity without clinical benefit", "Recommended only in young patients", "The preferred monotherapy"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "4-1", "ch": 4, "n": 1, "stem": "CAP diagnosis generally requires:", "opts": ["Fever alone", "Leukocytosis alone", "An opacity on chest imaging plus a compatible clinical syndrome", "A positive blood culture"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "4-2", "ch": 4, "n": 2, "stem": "If suspicion for CAP is high but the chest radiograph is negative, a reasonable next step is:", "opts": ["Chest CT in selected cases", "No further imaging", "MRI of the chest", "PET scan"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "4-3", "ch": 4, "n": 3, "stem": "The preferred severity score to supplement clinical judgment for CAP site-of-care decisions is:", "opts": ["Wells score", "qSOFA", "CHA2DS2-VASc", "Pneumonia Severity Index (PSI)"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "4-4", "ch": 4, "n": 4, "stem": "Patients in PSI class IV–V generally should:", "opts": ["Always be discharged home", "Be admitted to hospital, with ICU consideration", "Receive no antibiotics", "Be treated only with antivirals"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "4-5", "ch": 4, "n": 5, "stem": "In adults without underlying lung disease, a new room-air oxygen saturation &lt;92% suggests that:", "opts": ["Observation with reassessment or hospital admission is safer than waiting for &lt;90%", "Outpatient care without reassessment is safe", "It has no clinical significance", "ICU admission is mandatory"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "4-6", "ch": 4, "n": 6, "stem": "Major ICU criteria for severe CAP include:", "opts": ["Fever and cough", "Any leukocytosis", "Requirement for mechanical ventilation or vasopressor-requiring septic shock", "Any pleural effusion"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "4-7", "ch": 4, "n": 7, "stem": "For most outpatients with mild CAP, microbiologic testing is:", "opts": ["Always required (blood cultures and sputum culture)", "Required to choose antibiotics", "Required to diagnose CAP", "Generally not needed, except selected viral testing when it changes management"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "4-8", "ch": 4, "n": 8, "stem": "For most ward-admitted patients with moderate CAP, typical testing includes:", "opts": ["Stool culture", "Blood cultures, sputum Gram stain/culture, urine pneumococcal antigen, <em>Legionella</em> testing, and viral testing in season", "Skin biopsy", "Echocardiography only"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "4-9", "ch": 4, "n": 9, "stem": "MRSA nasal PCR/culture is obtained particularly when there are:", "opts": ["Mild outpatient CAP", "A patient request", "MRSA risk factors, severe disease, or a biphasic viral illness followed by deterioration", "Suspicion of <em>Legionella</em>"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "4-10", "ch": 4, "n": 10, "stem": "In severe CAP, bronchoscopic specimens are considered:", "opts": ["When feasible, balancing diagnostic benefit against procedural risks", "Never", "Only for outpatients", "Only after discharge"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "4-11", "ch": 4, "n": 11, "stem": "Cavitary pneumonia may warrant testing for:", "opts": ["Appendicitis", "Otitis media", "Cystitis", "Tuberculosis, fungal pathogens and <em>Nocardia</em>"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.4"]}, {"id": "4-12", "ch": 4, "n": 12, "stem": "Outpatient empiric CAP regimens are designed to cover:", "opts": ["Only viruses", "<em>S. pneumoniae</em>, <em>H. influenzae</em> and atypical pathogens", "Only MRSA", "Only <em>Pseudomonas</em>"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "4-13", "ch": 4, "n": 13, "stem": "Outpatients more likely to need expanded gram-negative/MSSA coverage include:", "opts": ["Older patients, smokers, and those with comorbidities or recent antibiotic use", "Healthy young adults with no comorbidities", "Patients with isolated sore throat", "Patients with seasonal allergies"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "4-14", "ch": 4, "n": 14, "stem": "ICU CAP is more likely to involve:", "opts": ["Only atypical pathogens", "Only anaerobes", "Resistant pathogens including CA-MRSA, and <em>Legionella</em>", "Only fungi"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "4-15", "ch": 4, "n": 15, "stem": "The strongest MRSA risk factors in CAP include:", "opts": ["Recent travel only", "Mild cough only", "A normal chest radiograph", "Known MRSA colonization, prior MRSA infection, or gram-positive cocci in clusters on sputum Gram stain"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "4-16", "ch": 4, "n": 16, "stem": "Which feature raises suspicion of MRSA pneumonia in severe CAP?", "opts": ["Rhinorrhea only", "Necrotizing or cavitary pneumonia", "Isolated pleuritic pain with normal imaging", "Bradycardia"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "4-17", "ch": 4, "n": 17, "stem": "Most patients with CAP should show some improvement within:", "opts": ["6 hours", "2 to 3 weeks", "48 to 72 hours", "2 to 3 months"], "a": 2, "sec": ["7.1"]}, {"id": "4-18", "ch": 4, "n": 18, "stem": "Failure to improve by 48–72 hours suggests:", "opts": ["Viral pneumonia only, in all cases", "That antibiotics should always be stopped immediately", "That no further evaluation is needed", "A resistant pathogen, a complication, or an incorrect diagnosis"], "a": 3, "sec": ["7.1"]}, {"id": "4-19", "ch": 4, "n": 19, "stem": "A routine follow-up chest radiograph after clinical resolution is:", "opts": ["Usually not required; radiographic response lags behind clinical response", "Always required", "Required within 24 hours", "Required for all patients under 30"], "a": 0, "sec": ["7.2"]}, {"id": "4-20", "ch": 4, "n": 20, "stem": "Vaccines that reduce CAP risk in adults include:", "opts": ["Rabies vaccine", "Influenza, COVID-19, pneumococcal and RSV vaccines (age/risk dependent)", "Yellow fever vaccine only", "Typhoid vaccine only"], "a": 1, "sec": ["8"]}, {"id": "5-1", "ch": 5, "n": 1, "stem": "HAP is defined as pneumonia occurring:", "opts": ["≥48 hours after admission and not incubating at admission", "Within 24 hours of admission", "Only in ventilated patients", "Only after discharge"], "a": 0, "sec": ["2.1"]}, {"id": "5-2", "ch": 5, "n": 2, "stem": "VAP is defined as pneumonia developing in an intubated patient on mechanical ventilation for:", "opts": ["≥12 hours", "≥24 hours", "<blockquote>\n<p>48 hours</p>\n</blockquote>", "<blockquote>\n<p>7 days only</p>\n</blockquote>"], "a": 2, "sec": ["2.1"]}, {"id": "5-3", "ch": 5, "n": 3, "stem": "VAP also includes HAP occurring within:", "opts": ["6 hours of extubation", "24 hours of extubation", "7 days of extubation", "48 hours of extubation"], "a": 3, "sec": ["2.1"]}, {"id": "5-4", "ch": 5, "n": 4, "stem": "The primary route of infection in HAP/VAP is:", "opts": ["Hematogenous spread from the skin", "Microaspiration of colonizing oropharyngeal organisms", "Tick bite", "Sexual transmission"], "a": 1, "sec": ["3"]}, {"id": "5-5", "ch": 5, "n": 5, "stem": "The most common HAP/VAP pathogens include:", "opts": ["<em>S. aureus</em> (including MRSA) and <em>P. aeruginosa</em>", "Only anaerobes", "Only <em>S. pneumoniae</em>", "Only fungi"], "a": 0, "sec": ["1"]}, {"id": "5-6", "ch": 5, "n": 6, "stem": "Major MDR risk factors include:", "opts": ["No prior antibiotics ever", "Age &lt;30 years", "Seasonal allergies", "Known MDR colonization, hospitalization ≥5 days and/or antibiotics in the prior 90 days"], "a": 3, "sec": ["2.2"]}, {"id": "5-7", "ch": 5, "n": 7, "stem": "The clinical diagnosis of HAP/VAP is based on:", "opts": ["A positive blood culture alone", "Procalcitonin alone", "A new infiltrate plus fever/purulent secretions/leukocytosis and decline in oxygenation", "CT scan only"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "5-8", "ch": 5, "n": 8, "stem": "VAP cannot be confirmed or excluded until:", "opts": ["The first dose of antibiotics is given", "Culture results are complete (often 2–3 days)", "The ventilator is removed", "A chest radiograph is normal"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "5-9", "ch": 5, "n": 9, "stem": "A positive respiratory culture with a normal chest radiograph suggests:", "opts": ["Tracheobronchitis", "Pulmonary embolism", "Pneumothorax", "Heart failure"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "5-10", "ch": 5, "n": 10, "stem": "The typical quantitative culture threshold supporting VAP for an endotracheal aspirate is:", "opts": ["10 cfu/mL", "1,000 cfu/mL", "10,000 cfu/mL", "≥1,000,000 cfu/mL"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "5-11", "ch": 5, "n": 11, "stem": "The typical quantitative culture threshold supporting VAP for BAL/mini-BAL is:", "opts": ["100 cfu/mL", "1,000 cfu/mL", "10,000 cfu/mL", "1,000,000 cfu/mL"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "5-12", "ch": 5, "n": 12, "stem": "The typical quantitative culture threshold supporting VAP for a protected specimen brush is:", "opts": ["10 cfu/mL", "1,000 cfu/mL", "10,000 cfu/mL", "1,000,000 cfu/mL"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "5-13", "ch": 5, "n": 13, "stem": "Empiric therapy selection should be informed by:", "opts": ["Prior cultures, recent antibiotics, local resistance, severity, Gram stain and MDR risk factors", "Only patient age", "Only the chest radiograph pattern", "Only procalcitonin"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "5-14", "ch": 5, "n": 14, "stem": "Severe HAP/VAP includes:", "opts": ["Any fever", "Mild leukocytosis only", "Normal oxygenation", "New septic shock (VAP/NV-HAP) and/or new need for ventilatory support (NV-HAP)"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "5-15", "ch": 5, "n": 15, "stem": "Reassessment for de-escalation should occur within:", "opts": ["6 hours", "12 hours", "48 to 72 hours", "24 hours"], "a": 2, "sec": ["7.1"]}, {"id": "5-16", "ch": 5, "n": 16, "stem": "If the MRSA nasal PCR/culture is negative, the suggested action is:", "opts": ["Continue anti-MRSA therapy for 14 days", "Stop empiric anti-MRSA therapy to reduce unnecessary antibiotic use", "Add a second anti-MRSA agent", "Switch to antifungal therapy"], "a": 1, "sec": ["7.2"]}, {"id": "5-17", "ch": 5, "n": 17, "stem": "The recommended duration of therapy for most HAP/VAP is:", "opts": ["7 days", "3 days", "5 days", "21 days"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.4"]}, {"id": "5-18", "ch": 5, "n": 18, "stem": "Courses longer than 7 days may be warranted for:", "opts": ["All patients", "Selected patients, such as those with empyema, lung abscess, slow response, immunocompromise or <em>S. aureus</em> bacteremia", "Only those with normal cultures", "Only those with viral pneumonia"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.4"]}, {"id": "5-19", "ch": 5, "n": 19, "stem": "Switching to oral therapy is generally appropriate when the patient is:", "opts": ["Still in shock", "Still worsening in oxygenation", "Hemodynamically stable, clinically improving and tolerating oral medications", "Unable to swallow"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.4"]}, {"id": "5-20", "ch": 5, "n": 20, "stem": "Which is an essential prevention practice for VAP/NV-HAP?", "opts": ["Routine chlorhexidine oral care", "Daily ventilator circuit changes", "Avoiding ventilator liberation protocols", "Head-of-bed elevation and oral care with toothbrushing (without chlorhexidine)"], "a": 3, "sec": ["8"]}, {"id": "6-1", "ch": 6, "n": 1, "stem": "Which mechanism allows a new influenza A virus subtype to emerge and cause a pandemic?", "opts": ["Antigenic drift due to point mutations in haemagglutinin", "Point mutations in neuraminidase causing oseltamivir resistance", "Antigenic shift due to reassortment of genome segments between different influenza A viruses", "Recombination between influenza A and influenza B viruses"], "a": 2, "sec": ["1.2"]}, {"id": "6-2", "ch": 6, "n": 2, "stem": "SARS-CoV-2 enters human respiratory cells mainly by binding of the spike protein to:", "opts": ["Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE2), after priming by TMPRSS2", "α2,6-linked sialic acid", "CD4 and the CCR5 co-receptor", "Intercellular adhesion molecule 1 (ICAM-1)"], "a": 0, "sec": ["1.3", "3.2"]}, {"id": "6-3", "ch": 6, "n": 3, "stem": "Which of the following groups is at the highest risk of severe RSV disease?", "opts": ["Healthy school-age children", "Healthy adults aged 20–40 years", "Adolescents with mild asthma", "Infants under 6 months (especially preterm) and adults aged 60 or older with chronic heart or lung disease"], "a": 3, "sec": ["2.3"]}, {"id": "6-4", "ch": 6, "n": 4, "stem": "Avian influenza A(H5N1) causes severe pneumonia in humans but spreads poorly from person to person mainly because:", "opts": ["It cannot replicate at human body temperature", "It preferentially binds α2,3-linked sialic acid, found mainly in the lower respiratory tract", "It lacks a neuraminidase protein", "It is completely neutralized by antibodies induced by seasonal vaccines"], "a": 1, "sec": ["3.1"]}, {"id": "6-5", "ch": 6, "n": 5, "stem": "A 24-year-old previously healthy man had influenza one week ago. After initial improvement he develops high fever, haemoptysis and septic shock. Chest CT shows multiple cavitating consolidations. Which pathogen is the most likely cause?", "opts": ["<em>Legionella pneumophila</em>", "<em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> (including community-acquired MRSA)", "<em>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</em>", "<em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>"], "a": 1, "sec": ["3.4", "4.2", "6.2"]}, {"id": "6-6", "ch": 6, "n": 6, "stem": "Which of the following defines <strong>severe</strong> COVID-19 in an adult?", "opts": ["Any pneumonia visible on chest CT", "Fever above 39 °C for more than 3 days", "The need for invasive mechanical ventilation", "Oxygen saturation below 90% on room air, respiratory rate above 30/min or signs of severe respiratory distress"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.3"]}, {"id": "6-7", "ch": 6, "n": 7, "stem": "The most common cause of bronchiolitis in infants is:", "opts": ["Respiratory syncytial virus", "Influenza B virus", "Adenovirus", "<em>Bordetella pertussis</em>"], "a": 0, "sec": ["4.4"]}, {"id": "6-8", "ch": 6, "n": 8, "stem": "In a patient hospitalized with suspected influenza, the preferred diagnostic test is:", "opts": ["A rapid antigen test, because a negative result excludes influenza", "Viral culture of a nasopharyngeal swab", "A molecular test (RT-PCR) on a respiratory sample", "A single serum sample for influenza antibodies"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "6-9", "ch": 6, "n": 9, "stem": "The most typical chest CT finding in COVID-19 pneumonia is:", "opts": ["Lobar consolidation with air bronchogram in a single lobe", "A thick-walled cavity in the upper lobe", "Bilateral, peripheral and basal ground-glass opacities", "A large unilateral pleural effusion"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "6-10", "ch": 6, "n": 10, "stem": "Which statement about corticosteroids in influenza pneumonia is correct?", "opts": ["They are not recommended, unless needed for another reason (e.g. COPD exacerbation, septic shock)", "They are recommended in all hospitalized patients who need oxygen", "They should replace antiviral therapy when symptoms started more than 48 hours earlier", "They reduce the risk of influenza-associated pulmonary aspergillosis"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "6-11", "ch": 6, "n": 11, "stem": "A 70-year-old woman is admitted with fever, cough and hypoxaemia during the influenza season. Symptoms started 4 days ago. The influenza PCR result will be available tomorrow. What is the best management?", "opts": ["No antiviral treatment, because more than 48 hours have passed since onset", "Wait for the PCR result before starting any antiviral", "Start amantadine, which is active against both influenza A and B", "Start oseltamivir now, without waiting for the test result"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "6-12", "ch": 6, "n": 12, "stem": "A 78-year-old man with diabetes has had mild COVID-19 symptoms for 2 days. His oxygen saturation is 96% on room air. Which treatment is most appropriate?", "opts": ["Dexamethasone", "Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir, after checking drug interactions", "Tocilizumab", "Convalescent plasma"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "6-13", "ch": 6, "n": 13, "stem": "Before prescribing nirmatrelvir-ritonavir to a kidney transplant recipient, the most important concern is:", "opts": ["Kidney toxicity", "Drug–drug interactions", "Interaction with food", "Viral resistance"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "6-14", "ch": 6, "n": 14, "stem": "A 60-year-old patient hospitalized with COVID-19 needs high-flow oxygen; CRP is rising. The recommended treatment includes:", "opts": ["Dexamethasone plus an IL-6 receptor blocker (e.g. tocilizumab) or baricitinib", "Ivermectin plus hydroxychloroquine", "Molnupiravir plus azithromycin", "Supportive care only, because immunomodulators are harmful"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "6-15", "ch": 6, "n": 15, "stem": "Regarding systemic corticosteroids in a patient with non-severe COVID-19 who does not need oxygen:", "opts": ["They are recommended for all patients older than 65 years", "They are recommended if CRP is raised", "They are recommended to prevent post-COVID-19 condition", "They are not recommended, because they may be harmful"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "6-16", "ch": 6, "n": 16, "stem": "A patient in the ICU with severe influenza on mechanical ventilation and corticosteroids worsens on day 6. BAL galactomannan is strongly positive. The most likely diagnosis is:", "opts": ["<em>Pneumocystis jirovecii</em> pneumonia", "Cytomegalovirus pneumonitis", "Influenza-associated pulmonary aspergillosis", "<em>Legionella</em> pneumonia"], "a": 2, "sec": ["3.4", "5.3"]}, {"id": "6-17", "ch": 6, "n": 17, "stem": "Post-COVID-19 condition is diagnosed when symptoms:", "opts": ["Usually start within 3 months of infection, last at least 2 months and cannot be explained by another diagnosis", "Persist for at least 2 weeks after a positive PCR test", "Occur only after severe disease requiring ICU admission", "Are accompanied by a persistently positive SARS-CoV-2 PCR"], "a": 0, "sec": ["7.2"]}, {"id": "6-18", "ch": 6, "n": 18, "stem": "A woman in the first trimester of pregnancy asks about influenza vaccination at the start of the influenza season. What is the correct advice?", "opts": ["Postpone vaccination until after delivery", "Vaccinate only in the third trimester", "Influenza vaccination is contraindicated in pregnancy", "Offer an inactivated influenza vaccine now: it is recommended in any trimester"], "a": 3, "sec": ["8.2"]}, {"id": "6-19", "ch": 6, "n": 19, "stem": "In the European Union, the maternal RSV vaccine (Abrysvo) is given during pregnancy at:", "opts": ["8–12 weeks of gestation", "14–20 weeks of gestation", "24–36 weeks of gestation", "After delivery, during breastfeeding"], "a": 2, "sec": ["8.6"]}, {"id": "6-20", "ch": 6, "n": 20, "stem": "How can newborns be protected against severe RSV disease during their first RSV season?", "opts": ["With a live attenuated RSV vaccine given at birth", "With passive immunization: maternal vaccination during pregnancy or a long-acting monoclonal antibody given to the infant", "With oseltamivir prophylaxis throughout the season", "No preventive measure is available for infants"], "a": 1, "sec": ["8.6"]}, {"id": "7-1", "ch": 7, "n": 1, "stem": "TB is most commonly transmitted via:", "opts": ["Inhalation of aerosol droplets from pulmonary/airway TB", "Fecal-oral spread", "Sexual transmission", "Tick bite"], "a": 0, "sec": ["2"]}, {"id": "7-2", "ch": 7, "n": 2, "stem": "TB infection (latent TB) is best defined as:", "opts": ["Positive sputum culture with symptoms", "Cavitary disease on chest radiograph", "Positive TST/IGRA without signs or symptoms of illness", "Hemoptysis with fever"], "a": 2, "sec": ["3.1"]}, {"id": "7-3", "ch": 7, "n": 3, "stem": "The lifetime risk of reactivation TB in immunocompetent individuals is approximately:", "opts": ["&lt;1%", "1 to 2%", "5 to 10%", "<blockquote>\n<p>50%</p>\n</blockquote>"], "a": 2, "sec": ["3.1"]}, {"id": "7-4", "ch": 7, "n": 4, "stem": "Reactivation TB most often involves:", "opts": ["Lower lobes only", "Pleura only", "Trachea only", "Apical and posterior segments of the upper lobes"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.1"]}, {"id": "7-5", "ch": 7, "n": 5, "stem": "Typical symptoms of reactivation TB include:", "opts": ["Acute watery diarrhea", "Cough, weight loss, fever, night sweats and/or hemoptysis", "Sudden unilateral leg swelling only", "Isolated dysuria"], "a": 1, "sec": ["4.1"]}, {"id": "7-6", "ch": 7, "n": 6, "stem": "Pulmonary TB should be suspected when there are compatible symptoms plus:", "opts": ["Epidemiologic risk such as exposure or residence/travel in endemic areas", "Recent shellfish ingestion", "Recent vaccination only", "Seasonal allergies"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "7-7", "ch": 7, "n": 7, "stem": "The definitive diagnosis of pulmonary TB is established by:", "opts": ["Positive IGRA alone", "Chest radiograph alone", "Leukocytosis", "Isolation of <em>M. tuberculosis</em> from a secretion/fluid or tissue"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "7-8", "ch": 7, "n": 8, "stem": "In most cases, a positive NAAT in an at-risk, untreated patient is considered:", "opts": ["Insufficient for diagnosis", "Proof of nontuberculous mycobacteria", "Sufficient for diagnosis of TB (with or without smear positivity)", "Only a screening test for latent TB"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "7-9", "ch": 7, "n": 9, "stem": "If imaging suggests pulmonary TB, the recommended sputum strategy is:", "opts": ["One sputum sample only", "Three sputum specimens ≥8 hours apart, including ≥1 early-morning specimen, for smear/culture/NAAT", "Two samples 1 hour apart", "No sputum testing if IGRA is positive"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "7-10", "ch": 7, "n": 10, "stem": "AFB smear sensitivity is limited because conventional light microscopy generally requires approximately:", "opts": ["10 bacilli/mL", "100 bacilli/mL", "1,000 bacilli/mL", "10,000 bacilli/mL"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "7-11", "ch": 7, "n": 11, "stem": "AFB seen on a smear may represent TB or NTM, so species identification requires:", "opts": ["Culture and/or NAAT", "Complete blood count", "ESR only", "Urinalysis"], "a": 0, "sec": ["1", "5.3"]}, {"id": "7-12", "ch": 7, "n": 12, "stem": "In suspected active TB, IGRA/TST:", "opts": ["Establish active TB if positive", "Support but cannot establish active TB; a negative result does not rule out disease", "Rule out active TB if negative", "Replace sputum testing"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.4"]}, {"id": "7-13", "ch": 7, "n": 13, "stem": "Testing for TB infection is generally reserved for:", "opts": ["Everyone, annually", "Only those with normal chest radiographs", "Only those with cough &lt;1 week", "Individuals at increased risk of developing TB disease who would benefit from treatment"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.6"]}, {"id": "7-14", "ch": 7, "n": 14, "stem": "In adults at low-to-intermediate risk of progression, IGRA is preferred over TST particularly when:", "opts": ["There are no prior vaccinations", "There are severe symptoms of TB disease", "There is prior BCG vaccination or the patient is unlikely to return for TST reading", "Cavitary disease is present"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.6"]}, {"id": "7-15", "ch": 7, "n": 15, "stem": "Before treating TB infection after a positive TST/IGRA, clinicians must:", "opts": ["Evaluate to exclude TB disease (history, exam, chest radiograph ± sputum testing)", "Start isoniazid immediately", "Repeat IGRA weekly for 1 month", "Obtain an abdominal CT"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.6"]}, {"id": "7-16", "ch": 7, "n": 16, "stem": "If symptoms and/or an abnormal chest radiograph are present after a positive TST/IGRA, the next step includes:", "opts": ["No further testing", "Stool culture", "Skin biopsy", "Three sputum specimens for AFB smear, culture and NAAT"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.6"]}, {"id": "7-17", "ch": 7, "n": 17, "stem": "Standard therapy for drug-susceptible pulmonary TB is organized into:", "opts": ["One phase only", "An intensive phase followed by a continuation phase", "Weekly pulses only", "Therapy only until fever resolves"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "7-18", "ch": 7, "n": 18, "stem": "The intensive phase of the traditional regimen includes:", "opts": ["Two drugs only", "Rifapentine and moxifloxacin only", "Isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol", "Ceftriaxone and azithromycin"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "7-19", "ch": 7, "n": 19, "stem": "DOT is recommended primarily to:", "opts": ["Ensure adherence and prevent the emergence of drug resistance", "Reduce cough immediately", "Replace public health reporting", "Avoid the need for sputum testing"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "7-20", "ch": 7, "n": 20, "stem": "For hospitalized patients with suspected pulmonary TB, appropriate infection control includes:", "opts": ["Contact precautions only", "Airborne infection isolation when possible; N95 for staff/visitors; surgical mask for the patient outside the AII room", "No isolation if smear-negative", "Gloves and gown only"], "a": 1, "sec": ["8.3"]}, {"id": "8-1", "ch": 8, "n": 1, "stem": "The most common cause of acute gastroenteritis across all age groups worldwide is:", "opts": ["Norovirus", "Rotavirus", "<em>Campylobacter jejuni</em>", "<em>Giardia duodenalis</em>"], "a": 0, "sec": ["1.1"]}, {"id": "8-2", "ch": 8, "n": 2, "stem": "Several guests develop vomiting and abdominal cramps 2 to 4 hours after eating cream pastries at a party. The most likely cause is:", "opts": ["Norovirus", "<em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> enterotoxin", "<em>Shigella sonnei</em>", "Shiga toxin-producing <em>E. coli</em>"], "a": 1, "sec": ["4.1"]}, {"id": "8-3", "ch": 8, "n": 3, "stem": "Cholera toxin causes diarrhea by:", "opts": ["Invading colonic epithelial cells", "Inactivating Rho-family GTPases", "Damaging glomerular endothelial cells", "ADP-ribosylating Gs, increasing cAMP and chloride secretion"], "a": 3, "sec": ["3.1"]}, {"id": "8-4", "ch": 8, "n": 4, "stem": "A 4-year-old develops bloody diarrhea after eating an undercooked hamburger. Six days later he is pale, with thrombocytopenia, schistocytes and acute kidney injury. The most likely pathogen is:", "opts": ["<em>Campylobacter jejuni</em>", "<em>Vibrio cholerae</em>", "Shiga toxin-producing <em>E. coli</em> O157:H7", "<em>Giardia duodenalis</em>"], "a": 2, "sec": ["3.1"]}, {"id": "8-5", "ch": 8, "n": 5, "stem": "In suspected STEC infection, antibiotics should be:", "opts": ["Given empirically to all patients", "Avoided, because they may increase the risk of HUS", "Given only as a fluoroquinolone", "Given only to adults"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "8-6", "ch": 8, "n": 6, "stem": "Guillain–Barré syndrome is a well-known complication of infection with:", "opts": ["<em>Campylobacter jejuni</em>", "Norovirus", "<em>Bacillus cereus</em>", "<em>Cryptosporidium</em>"], "a": 0, "sec": ["3.1"]}, {"id": "8-7", "ch": 8, "n": 7, "stem": "The cornerstone of treatment for acute watery diarrhea is:", "opts": ["Empiric fluoroquinolone therapy", "Loperamide for all patients", "Rehydration, preferably with reduced-osmolarity oral rehydration solution", "Prolonged fasting"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "8-8", "ch": 8, "n": 8, "stem": "Stool testing is indicated in a patient with acute diarrhea who has:", "opts": ["Two loose stools and no other symptoms", "Mild watery diarrhea for one day", "Nausea only", "Fever, bloody stools, severe abdominal pain or sepsis"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "8-9", "ch": 8, "n": 9, "stem": "<em>Clostridioides difficile</em> is best described as a:", "opts": ["Gram-negative, aerobic rod", "Protozoan cyst", "Gram-positive aerobic coccus", "Gram-positive, anaerobic, spore-forming bacillus producing toxins A and B"], "a": 3, "sec": ["1.2"]}, {"id": "8-10", "ch": 8, "n": 10, "stem": "The antibiotic classes most strongly associated with CDI are:", "opts": ["Tetracyclines and aminoglycosides", "Nitrofurantoin and fosfomycin", "Clindamycin, fluoroquinolones and cephalosporins", "Macrolides only"], "a": 2, "sec": ["2.2"]}, {"id": "8-11", "ch": 8, "n": 11, "stem": "Testing for CDI is appropriate in a hospitalized patient with:", "opts": ["≥3 unformed stools in 24 hours, with no laxatives in the previous 48 hours", "Formed stools and fever", "No symptoms, after completing CDI treatment", "Diarrhea starting the day after a laxative"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "8-12", "ch": 8, "n": 12, "stem": "A patient with diarrhea has a positive <em>C. difficile</em> NAAT and a negative toxin EIA. The best interpretation is:", "opts": ["CDI is excluded", "<em>C. difficile</em> is present but toxin is not detected: colonization or CDI, to be decided clinically", "Fulminant CDI", "Laboratory error, repeat testing daily"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "8-13", "ch": 8, "n": 13, "stem": "According to current guidelines, the preferred treatment for an initial CDI episode is:", "opts": ["IV vancomycin", "Oral metronidazole", "Fidaxomicin", "Loperamide plus probiotics"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "8-14", "ch": 8, "n": 14, "stem": "IV vancomycin is not used to treat CDI because:", "opts": ["<em>C. difficile</em> is resistant to vancomycin", "It causes toxic megacolon", "It is more expensive than oral vancomycin", "It does not reach the colonic lumen"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "8-15", "ch": 8, "n": 15, "stem": "A patient with CDI develops hypotension, abdominal distension and ileus. The most appropriate management is:", "opts": ["Oral metronidazole alone", "Oral/rectal vancomycin plus IV metronidazole, with early surgical consultation", "Loperamide and fluids", "Wait for repeat stool testing"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "8-16", "ch": 8, "n": 16, "stem": "After successful treatment of CDI, a \"test of cure\":", "opts": ["Is not recommended, because toxin and NAAT may remain positive", "Is mandatory before discharge", "Should be done weekly for 8 weeks", "Should be done only with toxin EIA"], "a": 0, "sec": ["7.2"]}, {"id": "8-17", "ch": 8, "n": 17, "stem": "The recommended treatment for a second or later CDI recurrence is:", "opts": ["Repeat oral metronidazole", "Fecal microbiota transplantation after antibiotic therapy", "IV vancomycin", "No treatment"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "8-18", "ch": 8, "n": 18, "stem": "During a CDI outbreak, the best hand hygiene method is:", "opts": ["Alcohol-based hand rub only", "No hand hygiene if gloves are worn", "Washing with soap and water", "Chlorhexidine rub only"], "a": 2, "sec": ["8.2"]}, {"id": "8-19", "ch": 8, "n": 19, "stem": "Which statement about rotavirus vaccine is correct?", "opts": ["It is a live oral vaccine given in infancy and recommended for all national programmes", "It is an injectable toxoid vaccine for adults", "It is given only to travelers", "It also protects against norovirus"], "a": 0, "sec": ["8.1"]}, {"id": "8-20", "ch": 8, "n": 20, "stem": "Antimotility agents such as loperamide:", "opts": ["Are recommended for all children with diarrhea", "Are first-line therapy for CDI", "Are safe in bloody diarrhea with high fever", "Should be avoided in bloody diarrhea, suspected STEC and CDI"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "9-1", "ch": 9, "n": 1, "stem": "The most common cause of acute uncomplicated cystitis in young women is:", "opts": ["<em>Staphylococcus saprophyticus</em>", "<em>Escherichia coli</em>", "<em>Enterococcus faecalis</em>", "<em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>"], "a": 1, "sec": ["1.1"]}, {"id": "9-2", "ch": 9, "n": 2, "stem": "A urease-producing organism associated with alkaline urine and struvite stones is:", "opts": ["<em>Escherichia coli</em>", "<em>Enterococcus faecalis</em>", "Group B <em>Streptococcus</em>", "<em>Proteus mirabilis</em>"], "a": 3, "sec": ["1.2"]}, {"id": "9-3", "ch": 9, "n": 3, "stem": "In most urinary tract infections, bacteria reach the bladder and kidney:", "opts": ["By ascending from the periurethral area through the urethra", "Through the bloodstream during bacteremia", "Through the lymphatic vessels", "By direct spread from the bowel wall"], "a": 0, "sec": ["3.1"]}, {"id": "9-4", "ch": 9, "n": 4, "stem": "A urinary nitrite test may be negative despite a true UTI caused by:", "opts": ["<em>Escherichia coli</em>", "<em>Klebsiella pneumoniae</em>", "<em>Enterococcus faecalis</em>", "<em>Proteus mirabilis</em>"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "9-5", "ch": 9, "n": 5, "stem": "In an asymptomatic non-pregnant woman, asymptomatic bacteriuria is defined as:", "opts": ["≥10³ cfu/mL in a single voided specimen", "Pyuria on dipstick alone", "≥10⁵ cfu/mL of the same organism in two consecutive voided specimens", "Any bacterial growth in a voided specimen"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "9-6", "ch": 9, "n": 6, "stem": "Screening for and treatment of asymptomatic bacteriuria is recommended in:", "opts": ["Pregnant women", "Older residents of long-term care facilities", "Women with diabetes", "Patients with long-term urinary catheters"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.6"]}, {"id": "9-7", "ch": 9, "n": 7, "stem": "Recurrent UTI is defined as:", "opts": ["≥1 infection in 12 months", "≥2 infections in 6 months or ≥3 infections in 12 months", "≥4 infections in 6 months", "≥2 infections in 24 months"], "a": 1, "sec": ["2.1"]}, {"id": "9-8", "ch": 9, "n": 8, "stem": "Which drug is NOT appropriate for acute pyelonephritis?", "opts": ["Ceftriaxone", "Ciprofloxacin (if the isolate is susceptible)", "Piperacillin-tazobactam", "Nitrofurantoin"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "9-9", "ch": 9, "n": 9, "stem": "A first-line option for acute uncomplicated cystitis is:", "opts": ["Ciprofloxacin", "Amoxicillin", "Ceftriaxone", "Fosfomycin trometamol"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "9-10", "ch": 9, "n": 10, "stem": "A patient with pyelonephritis still has fever after 72 hours of appropriate therapy. The best next step is:", "opts": ["Continue the same therapy without investigations", "Imaging (preferably CT) to exclude obstruction, abscess or emphysematous pyelonephritis", "Stop all antibiotics", "Repeat the dipstick only"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.4", "7.2"]}, {"id": "9-11", "ch": 9, "n": 11, "stem": "The culture threshold for CAUTI in a symptomatic catheterized patient is:", "opts": ["≥10³ cfu/mL", "≥10⁵ cfu/mL in two consecutive samples", "≥10⁸ cfu/mL", "Pyuria alone, without culture"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "9-12", "ch": 9, "n": 12, "stem": "A patient with a urinary catheter in place for 3 weeks develops fever. Before starting antibiotics, the urine sample should be collected:", "opts": ["From the drainage bag", "By aspirating the old catheter tubing", "From a newly inserted catheter, after replacing the old one", "No sample is needed in catheterized patients"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "9-13", "ch": 9, "n": 13, "stem": "According to current guidelines, a patient with complicated UTI who is improving on effective therapy should receive:", "opts": ["5–7 days of a fluoroquinolone or 7 days of a non-fluoroquinolone, even with gram-negative bacteremia", "14 days in all cases", "21 days if blood cultures are positive", "A single dose"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.4"]}, {"id": "9-14", "ch": 9, "n": 14, "stem": "A man with febrile UTI and suspected acute bacterial prostatitis may need a treatment duration of:", "opts": ["A single dose", "3 days", "5 days", "10–14 days"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.4"]}, {"id": "9-15", "ch": 9, "n": 15, "stem": "Emphysematous pyelonephritis typically occurs in patients with:", "opts": ["Recurrent cystitis in young healthy women", "Poorly controlled diabetes mellitus", "Asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy", "Short-term urinary catheters"], "a": 1, "sec": ["4.3"]}, {"id": "9-16", "ch": 9, "n": 16, "stem": "According to current evidence, cranberry products show little or no benefit in:", "opts": ["Children", "Women with recurrent UTI", "Institutionalized older people, pregnant women and patients with neurogenic bladder", "People susceptible to UTI after medical interventions"], "a": 2, "sec": ["8.1"]}, {"id": "9-17", "ch": 9, "n": 17, "stem": "Which of the following is NOT a non-antibiotic measure with some efficacy in preventing recurrent UTI in women?", "opts": ["Vaginal estrogen in postmenopausal women", "Methenamine hippurate", "Routine screening and treatment of asymptomatic bacteriuria", "Immunoactive prophylaxis with bacterial lysates"], "a": 2, "sec": ["8.1"]}, {"id": "9-18", "ch": 9, "n": 18, "stem": "<em>Staphylococcus aureus</em> is isolated from the urine of a febrile patient without a urinary catheter. The best interpretation is:", "opts": ["Possible hematogenous seeding; blood cultures should be obtained", "Always contamination", "The typical cause of uncomplicated cystitis", "No further action is needed"], "a": 0, "sec": ["1.1"]}, {"id": "9-19", "ch": 9, "n": 19, "stem": "Which antibiotic class is the treatment of choice for severe infections caused by ESBL-producing Enterobacterales?", "opts": ["Third-generation cephalosporins", "Penicillins", "Macrolides", "Carbapenems"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "9-20", "ch": 9, "n": 20, "stem": "The most effective measure to prevent CAUTI is:", "opts": ["Routine systemic antibiotic prophylaxis", "Avoiding unnecessary catheters and removing them as soon as possible", "Changing catheters at fixed weekly intervals", "Bladder irrigation with antimicrobials"], "a": 1, "sec": ["8.2"]}, {"id": "10-1", "ch": 10, "n": 1, "stem": "The most common cause of nonpurulent cellulitis in an immunocompetent adult is:", "opts": ["<em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>", "β-hemolytic streptococci", "<em>Escherichia coli</em>", "<em>Clostridium perfringens</em>"], "a": 1, "sec": ["1.1"]}, {"id": "10-2", "ch": 10, "n": 2, "stem": "According to the FDA definition, ABSSSI requires a minimum lesion area of:", "opts": ["5 cm²", "25 cm²", "50 cm²", "75 cm²"], "a": 3, "sec": ["2.1"]}, {"id": "10-3", "ch": 10, "n": 3, "stem": "Compared with cellulitis, erysipelas is characterized by:", "opts": ["A raised, sharply demarcated border, reflecting involvement of the upper dermis and superficial lymphatics", "Involvement of the deep fascia", "A predominance of gram-negative bacilli", "A slow onset without fever"], "a": 0, "sec": ["4.1"]}, {"id": "10-4", "ch": 10, "n": 4, "stem": "Which finding most strongly suggests necrotizing fasciitis rather than simple cellulitis?", "opts": ["Warmth over the lesion", "Low-grade fever", "Pain out of proportion to the findings, with areas of skin anesthesia", "Tender inguinal lymphadenopathy"], "a": 2, "sec": ["4.3"]}, {"id": "10-5", "ch": 10, "n": 5, "stem": "A 78-year-old afebrile woman with chronic venous insufficiency has red, scaly, itchy skin on both lower legs for several weeks. The most likely diagnosis is:", "opts": ["Bilateral streptococcal cellulitis", "Necrotizing fasciitis", "Stasis dermatitis", "Gas gangrene"], "a": 2, "sec": ["4.5"]}, {"id": "10-6", "ch": 10, "n": 6, "stem": "In a patient with typical uncomplicated cellulitis, blood cultures are:", "opts": ["Not routinely recommended; reserved for situations such as neutropenia, chemotherapy, immersion injuries or animal bites", "Mandatory before any antibiotic", "Positive in most cases", "The best way to identify MRSA"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "10-7", "ch": 10, "n": 7, "stem": "The most useful bedside test to detect an occult abscess within an area of cellulitis is:", "opts": ["Plain radiograph", "Skin swab culture", "Antistreptolysin O titer", "Point-of-care ultrasound"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "10-8", "ch": 10, "n": 8, "stem": "The LRINEC score includes:", "opts": ["Procalcitonin, lactate and platelet count", "C-reactive protein, white cell count, hemoglobin, sodium, creatinine and glucose", "Creatine kinase, myoglobin and potassium", "Blood culture results and wound Gram stain"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.4"]}, {"id": "10-9", "ch": 10, "n": 9, "stem": "The gold standard for diagnosing necrotizing soft tissue infection is:", "opts": ["Surgical exploration", "A LRINEC score ≥6", "MRI of the limb", "Blood cultures"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.4"]}, {"id": "10-10", "ch": 10, "n": 10, "stem": "The primary treatment of an uncomplicated skin abscess is:", "opts": ["Oral penicillin alone", "Topical antibiotics", "Incision and drainage", "Warm compresses only"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "10-11", "ch": 10, "n": 11, "stem": "In a drained skin abscess, in a setting with frequent MRSA, adding TMP-SMX or clindamycin:", "opts": ["Increases mortality", "Has no effect on any outcome", "Is needed only in children", "Modestly increases the clinical cure rate"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "10-12", "ch": 10, "n": 12, "stem": "The preferred treatment for mild nonpurulent cellulitis in an outpatient is:", "opts": ["IV vancomycin", "An oral anti-streptococcal β-lactam", "Oral ciprofloxacin", "Incision and drainage"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "10-13", "ch": 10, "n": 13, "stem": "In patients with uncomplicated nonpurulent cellulitis, adding TMP-SMX to cephalexin:", "opts": ["Doubled the cure rate", "Did not improve the per-protocol cure rate", "Was required because MRSA is the main cause", "Shortened the duration of therapy to 1 day"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "10-14", "ch": 10, "n": 14, "stem": "For uncomplicated cellulitis that improves, the recommended duration of antibiotic therapy is:", "opts": ["1 day", "3 weeks", "6 weeks", "5–6 days, extended if the infection has not improved"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.5"]}, {"id": "10-15", "ch": 10, "n": 15, "stem": "Clindamycin is added in necrotizing fasciitis due to group A <em>Streptococcus</em> mainly because it:", "opts": ["Suppresses bacterial toxin production", "Covers <em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>", "Penetrates the cerebrospinal fluid", "Replaces the need for surgery"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.6"]}, {"id": "10-16", "ch": 10, "n": 16, "stem": "A man with cirrhosis develops hemorrhagic bullae and septic shock 24 hours after cutting his leg while fishing in the sea. The most likely pathogen is:", "opts": ["<em>Pasteurella multocida</em>", "<em>Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae</em>", "<em>Vibrio vulnificus</em>", "<em>Aeromonas hydrophila</em>"], "a": 2, "sec": ["1.2"]}, {"id": "10-17", "ch": 10, "n": 17, "stem": "The empiric oral antibiotic of choice for an infected dog bite is:", "opts": ["Cephalexin", "Clindamycin alone", "Amoxicillin-clavulanate", "Metronidazole alone"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.7"]}, {"id": "10-18", "ch": 10, "n": 18, "stem": "Twenty-four hours after starting appropriate antibiotics for cellulitis, the erythema has slightly spread beyond the pen mark, but the patient is afebrile and feels better. The best interpretation is:", "opts": ["Early increase in erythema can occur and is not, by itself, treatment failure; reassess", "Immediate switch to a carbapenem is needed", "Necrotizing fasciitis is confirmed", "The diagnosis of cellulitis is excluded"], "a": 0, "sec": ["7.1"]}, {"id": "10-19", "ch": 10, "n": 19, "stem": "In a patient with chronic leg edema and recurrent cellulitis, which measure reduces recurrences?", "opts": ["Long-term topical mupirocin on the leg", "Weekly blood cultures", "Bed rest", "Compression therapy"], "a": 3, "sec": ["8"]}, {"id": "10-20", "ch": 10, "n": 20, "stem": "Regarding vaccines and skin infections:", "opts": ["A group A streptococcal vaccine is routinely recommended", "No licensed vaccine exists against group A <em>Streptococcus</em> or <em>S. aureus</em>; tetanus status should be checked after wounds", "<em>S. aureus</em> vaccination prevents recurrent abscesses", "Pneumococcal vaccine prevents erysipelas"], "a": 1, "sec": ["8"]}, {"id": "11-1", "ch": 11, "n": 1, "stem": "The most common cause of osteomyelitis in all age groups is:", "opts": ["<em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>", "<em>Escherichia coli</em>", "<em>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</em>", "<em>Mycobacterium tuberculosis</em>"], "a": 0, "sec": ["1.1"]}, {"id": "11-2", "ch": 11, "n": 2, "stem": "A 2-year-old child has osteomyelitis with negative standard cultures. Which organism is most likely and best detected by PCR?", "opts": ["<em>Salmonella enterica</em>", "<em>Kingella kingae</em>", "<em>Haemophilus influenzae</em> type b", "<em>Candida albicans</em>"], "a": 1, "sec": ["1.1", "1.2"]}, {"id": "11-3", "ch": 11, "n": 3, "stem": "Which association between host and pathogen is classic?", "opts": ["Diabetic foot and <em>Kingella kingae</em>", "Puncture wound through a shoe and <em>Brucella</em>", "Infant and <em>Cutibacterium acnes</em>", "Sickle cell disease and <em>Salmonella</em>"], "a": 3, "sec": ["1.1"]}, {"id": "11-4", "ch": 11, "n": 4, "stem": "Three weeks after a knee replacement, a patient has wound drainage and a painful but <strong>well-fixed</strong> prosthesis. Joint aspirate grows rifampicin-susceptible <em>S. aureus</em>. The most appropriate strategy is:", "opts": ["Oral antibiotics alone, without surgery", "Two-stage exchange in all cases", "Debridement, antibiotics and implant retention (DAIR), with a rifampicin-containing regimen", "Amputation"], "a": 2, "sec": ["3.5", "6.7"]}, {"id": "11-5", "ch": 11, "n": 5, "stem": "Which finding alone confirms a prosthetic joint infection?", "opts": ["A raised CRP", "Fever", "A positive culture from a swab of the wound surface", "A sinus tract communicating with the prosthesis"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.5", "5.7"]}, {"id": "11-6", "ch": 11, "n": 6, "stem": "In the pathogenesis of chronic osteomyelitis, a \"sequestrum\" is:", "opts": ["A fragment of necrotic bone that harbors bacteria in biofilm", "New bone formed around the infection", "A sinus tract draining to the skin", "The growth plate of a child"], "a": 0, "sec": ["3.2"]}, {"id": "11-7", "ch": 11, "n": 7, "stem": "Why does pyogenic spondylodiscitis typically involve two adjacent vertebrae and the disc between them?", "opts": ["The disc is richly vascularized in adults", "Each segmental artery supplies the endplates of two adjacent vertebrae", "Bacteria spread through the cerebrospinal fluid", "Infection always starts in the spinous process"], "a": 1, "sec": ["3.4"]}, {"id": "11-8", "ch": 11, "n": 8, "stem": "Which feature favors tuberculous over pyogenic spondylitis?", "opts": ["Acute onset over a few days", "Early destruction of the disc", "Isolated lumbar involvement with small abscess", "Thoracic involvement, relative disc sparing and large \"cold\" abscess"], "a": 3, "sec": ["3.4", "4.2"]}, {"id": "11-9", "ch": 11, "n": 9, "stem": "The most common symptom of vertebral osteomyelitis is:", "opts": ["Localized back or neck pain", "High fever in all patients", "Paraplegia", "Skin rash"], "a": 0, "sec": ["4.2"]}, {"id": "11-10", "ch": 11, "n": 10, "stem": "A patient with spondylodiscitis develops new leg weakness and urinary retention. The most appropriate next step is:", "opts": ["Outpatient physiotherapy", "Repeat CRP in 2 weeks", "Urgent MRI and surgical evaluation for epidural abscess", "Oral analgesics and discharge"], "a": 2, "sec": ["4.2", "6.3"]}, {"id": "11-11", "ch": 11, "n": 11, "stem": "The imaging test of choice for suspected vertebral osteomyelitis is:", "opts": ["Plain X-ray", "MRI with gadolinium", "Ultrasound", "Bone densitometry"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "11-12", "ch": 11, "n": 12, "stem": "In suspected vertebral osteomyelitis, an image-guided biopsy is usually NOT needed when:", "opts": ["Blood cultures grow <em>S. aureus</em> and MRI is compatible", "CRP is elevated", "The patient has back pain at night", "Plain X-ray is normal"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.4"]}, {"id": "11-13", "ch": 11, "n": 13, "stem": "A stable, neurologically intact patient has MRI findings of spondylodiscitis and negative blood cultures. The best next step is:", "opts": ["Start empiric antibiotics for 6 weeks without further testing", "Start corticosteroids", "Image-guided needle biopsy for culture and histology before antibiotics", "Repeat MRI in 6 months"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.4", "6.1"]}, {"id": "11-14", "ch": 11, "n": 14, "stem": "Cultures from a superficial swab of a chronic sinus tract:", "opts": ["Are the gold standard for chronic osteomyelitis", "Are always sterile", "Replace bone biopsy in diabetic foot osteomyelitis", "Are unreliable, because they reflect colonization rather than bone pathogens"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.5"]}, {"id": "11-15", "ch": 11, "n": 15, "stem": "For pyogenic vertebral osteomyelitis, 6 weeks of antibiotics compared with 12 weeks are:", "opts": ["Inferior, with more relapses", "Equally effective for cure", "Associated with more deaths", "Only acceptable for MRSA"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "11-16", "ch": 11, "n": 16, "stem": "Which patient with hematogenous vertebral osteomyelitis is a candidate for a longer (at least 8 weeks) course?", "opts": ["A patient with MSSA infection and no abscess", "A patient with streptococcal infection and normal renal function", "A patient with MRSA infection and an undrained psoas abscess", "A patient whose CRP normalized after 2 weeks"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "11-17", "ch": 11, "n": 17, "stem": "For bone and joint infection, early oral therapy compared with intravenous therapy is:", "opts": ["Equally effective, with fewer catheter complications", "Inferior in all subgroups", "Only acceptable in children", "Associated with more treatment failures"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "11-18", "ch": 11, "n": 18, "stem": "Which is an indication for surgery in vertebral osteomyelitis?", "opts": ["Fever on day 2 of therapy", "Mild back pain improving with treatment", "Elevated ESR at diagnosis", "Progressive neurologic deficit or spinal instability"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "11-19", "ch": 11, "n": 19, "stem": "In a patient with a diabetic foot ulcer, which combination is recommended as the initial diagnostic approach to osteomyelitis?", "opts": ["Swab culture and procalcitonin only", "Probe-to-bone test, inflammatory markers (ESR) and plain X-ray", "Bone scintigraphy only", "Clinical impression only"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.6"]}, {"id": "11-20", "ch": 11, "n": 20, "stem": "In a patient improving clinically during treatment for spondylodiscitis, which statement about follow-up is correct?", "opts": ["MRI should be repeated every 2 weeks", "Antibiotics should be stopped when MRI normalizes", "Routine repeat MRI is not recommended, because MRI changes can persist or look worse despite a good response", "Follow-up can stop at the end of therapy"], "a": 2, "sec": ["7"]}, {"id": "12-1", "ch": 12, "n": 1, "stem": "Brucellosis is best described as:", "opts": ["A human-only infection spread by respiratory droplets", "A tick-borne protozoal infection", "A zoonotic infection transmitted from infected animals via food ingestion or contact with tissues or fluids", "A helminth infection acquired from freshwater snails"], "a": 2, "sec": ["2"]}, {"id": "12-2", "ch": 12, "n": 2, "stem": "The most common mechanism of transmission to humans is:", "opts": ["Consumption of unpasteurized dairy products", "Mosquito bite", "Sexual transmission", "Fecal-oral spread from humans"], "a": 0, "sec": ["2"]}, {"id": "12-3", "ch": 12, "n": 3, "stem": "Which occupational group is classically at risk for brucellosis?", "opts": ["Office workers", "Retail cashiers", "School teachers", "Abattoir workers"], "a": 3, "sec": ["2"]}, {"id": "12-4", "ch": 12, "n": 4, "stem": "Which statement about human-to-human transmission is most accurate?", "opts": ["It is the dominant route worldwide", "It is rare but has been described (e.g. transfusion, breastfeeding, sexual contact, congenital)", "It never occurs", "It occurs only via respiratory droplets"], "a": 1, "sec": ["2"]}, {"id": "12-5", "ch": 12, "n": 5, "stem": "The typical incubation period is:", "opts": ["2 to 4 weeks (sometimes months)", "1 to 3 days", "6 to 12 months", "Always more than 1 year"], "a": 0, "sec": ["4.1"]}, {"id": "12-6", "ch": 12, "n": 6, "stem": "A typical symptom constellation includes:", "opts": ["Acute watery diarrhea and rash", "Severe jaundice within 24 hours", "Hemoptysis and pleuritic chest pain only", "Insidious fever, malaise, night sweats and arthralgias"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.1"]}, {"id": "12-7", "ch": 12, "n": 7, "stem": "Which physical finding may be observed but is nonspecific?", "opts": ["Pathognomonic eschar", "Koplik spots", "Hepatomegaly and/or splenomegaly", "Meningismus in all cases"], "a": 2, "sec": ["4.1"]}, {"id": "12-8", "ch": 12, "n": 8, "stem": "The most common focal complication is:", "opts": ["Endocarditis", "Osteoarticular disease", "Neurobrucellosis", "Pulmonary abscess"], "a": 1, "sec": ["4.2"]}, {"id": "12-9", "ch": 12, "n": 9, "stem": "Sacroiliitis in brucellosis is:", "opts": ["Bilateral in nearly all cases", "Always painless", "Always cervical", "Often unilateral, and an important manifestation in young adults"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.2"]}, {"id": "12-10", "ch": 12, "n": 10, "stem": "Endocarditis in brucellosis is best characterized as:", "opts": ["Uncommon, but the main cause of death attributable to brucellosis", "Common and benign", "Always right-sided", "Never involves the aortic valve"], "a": 0, "sec": ["4.2"]}, {"id": "12-11", "ch": 12, "n": 11, "stem": "A definitive diagnosis can be made by:", "opts": ["A single low-titer serology in any setting", "PCR only, without clinical context", "Culture from blood, body fluids or tissue, OR a ≥4-fold rise in antibody titer on paired sera", "Imaging alone"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "12-12", "ch": 12, "n": 12, "stem": "A presumptive diagnosis may be made by:", "opts": ["SAT titer below 1:40", "SAT titer ≥1:160 after symptom onset", "Negative blood cultures", "Normal liver enzymes"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "12-13", "ch": 12, "n": 13, "stem": "Initial diagnostic studies for suspected brucellosis should include:", "opts": ["Blood cultures and serologic testing", "Stool culture only", "Skin biopsy", "Urine antigen only"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "12-14", "ch": 12, "n": 14, "stem": "Which statement about culture is most accurate?", "opts": ["Culture is never useful", "Bone marrow culture is less sensitive than blood culture", "Blood culture is always positive in chronic disease", "Culture is the gold standard and proves active infection, but its sensitivity varies"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "12-15", "ch": 12, "n": 15, "stem": "Compared with blood culture, bone marrow culture is:", "opts": ["Less sensitive and slower", "Never positive", "More sensitive and less affected by prior antibiotics", "Only useful in children"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "12-16", "ch": 12, "n": 16, "stem": "Which statement about serology is most accurate?", "opts": ["Serology never cross-reacts with other bacteria", "Serology must be interpreted in clinical and epidemiologic context, and SAT can be falsely negative in chronic infection", "SAT is always positive in chronic infection", "ELISA is never used"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.4"]}, {"id": "12-17", "ch": 12, "n": 17, "stem": "Key treatment principles include:", "opts": ["Combination therapy and prolonged duration to reduce relapse", "Monotherapy for 7 days is sufficient", "No antibiotics are needed", "Only topical therapy is effective"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "12-18", "ch": 12, "n": 18, "stem": "Which of the following antibiotic combinations is NOT used to treat brucellosis?", "opts": ["Doxycycline plus an aminoglycoside", "Doxycycline plus rifampicin", "Ceftriaxone plus rifampicin plus doxycycline", "Ampicillin plus cefazolin"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.2", "6.3"]}, {"id": "12-19", "ch": 12, "n": 19, "stem": "Relapse most commonly occurs:", "opts": ["Within 24 hours", "Within 6 months after the end of treatment (up to 12 months)", "Only after 10 years", "Only during therapy"], "a": 1, "sec": ["7"]}, {"id": "12-20", "ch": 12, "n": 20, "stem": "Which prevention statement is most accurate?", "opts": ["A human vaccine is widely available", "Only mosquito control matters", "Avoid raw-milk dairy products, use occupational precautions; there is no human vaccine", "Postexposure guidance is unnecessary after a laboratory exposure"], "a": 2, "sec": ["8"]}, {"id": "13-1", "ch": 13, "n": 1, "stem": "Which statement best describes the <em>Leishmania</em> form found in human tissue?", "opts": ["Intracellular amastigotes in macrophages", "Extracellular promastigotes in plasma", "Intracellular sporozoites in hepatocytes", "Extracellular trophozoites in the intestinal lumen"], "a": 0, "sec": ["1"]}, {"id": "13-2", "ch": 13, "n": 2, "stem": "In the sand fly, ingested amastigotes develop into promastigotes over:", "opts": ["4 to 14 hours", "24 to 48 hours", "4 to 14 days", "4 to 14 weeks"], "a": 2, "sec": ["1"]}, {"id": "13-3", "ch": 13, "n": 3, "stem": "The most typical lesion evolution in localized cutaneous leishmaniasis is:", "opts": ["Painful vesicles → dermatomal crusting", "Rapidly necrotic eschar with severe pain within 24 hours", "Diffuse urticaria with angioedema", "Papule → nodule/plaque → usually painless ulcer with an indurated border"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.1"]}, {"id": "13-4", "ch": 13, "n": 4, "stem": "Which finding is consistent with cutaneous leishmaniasis?", "opts": ["Acute onset with resolution in 48 hours", "Nodular lymphangitis and regional lymphadenopathy", "Prominent neuropathy as a defining feature", "Always intensely painful ulcers"], "a": 1, "sec": ["4.1"]}, {"id": "13-5", "ch": 13, "n": 5, "stem": "In the Western hemisphere, mucosal leishmaniasis is most often caused by:", "opts": ["<em>Leishmania</em> subgenus <em>Leishmania</em> only", "<em>Trypanosoma cruzi</em>", "<em>Leishmania</em> (<em>Viannia</em>) species", "<em>Plasmodium falciparum</em>"], "a": 2, "sec": ["4.2"]}, {"id": "13-6", "ch": 13, "n": 6, "stem": "Which symptom is characteristic of mucosal leishmaniasis?", "opts": ["Nasal stuffiness or blockage with mucosal bleeding", "Pleuritic chest pain", "Hematuria with flank pain", "Acute watery diarrhea only"], "a": 0, "sec": ["4.2"]}, {"id": "13-7", "ch": 13, "n": 7, "stem": "The classic syndrome of visceral leishmaniasis (kala-azar) most commonly includes:", "opts": ["Acute fever with rash and arthralgia over 24 hours", "Isolated cervical lymphadenitis without systemic symptoms", "Purely neurologic deficits without systemic findings", "Subacute malaise, fever, weight loss and splenomegaly over weeks to months"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.3"]}, {"id": "13-8", "ch": 13, "n": 8, "stem": "Definitive diagnosis of cutaneous leishmaniasis requires:", "opts": ["Clinical appearance alone", "Demonstration of the parasite in a clinical specimen by histology, culture or PCR", "Serology", "Response to empiric antibiotics"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "13-9", "ch": 13, "n": 9, "stem": "To maximize diagnostic yield in CL, the preferred initial approach (if feasible) is:", "opts": ["Histology, culture and PCR on the initial sample", "Serology only", "Culture only", "No testing if the lesion is painless"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "13-10", "ch": 13, "n": 10, "stem": "Which statement about serology in cutaneous leishmaniasis is most accurate?", "opts": ["It is the preferred confirmatory test", "It reliably distinguishes active from past infection", "It is required before biopsy", "Commercially available serology has no clinical role in diagnosing CL"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "13-11", "ch": 13, "n": 11, "stem": "For CL, which site is preferred for a punch biopsy of an ulcerative lesion?", "opts": ["The center of the ulcer base only", "Normal-appearing distant skin", "The raised border of the ulcer", "The overlying eschar, without debridement"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "13-12", "ch": 13, "n": 12, "stem": "On histopathology, which feature is essential to identify <em>Leishmania</em> amastigotes?", "opts": ["Budding yeast forms", "Visualization of the kinetoplast", "Acid-fast bacilli", "Septate hyphae"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "13-13", "ch": 13, "n": 13, "stem": "A rapid bedside diagnostic method for CL is:", "opts": ["KOH preparation for hyphae", "Latex agglutination for cryptococcal antigen", "Thick and thin blood smears for malaria", "Tzanck-type smear of lesion scraping stained with Wright-Giemsa"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "13-14", "ch": 13, "n": 14, "stem": "Which condition is a recognized mimic of cutaneous leishmaniasis?", "opts": ["Sporotrichosis", "Acute appendicitis", "Acute otitis media", "Myasthenia gravis"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "13-15", "ch": 13, "n": 15, "stem": "Among the mimics of CL, which feature favors leprosy?", "opts": ["Chronicity", "Location on exposed skin", "Neuropathy", "Indurated ulcer border"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "13-16", "ch": 13, "n": 16, "stem": "Definitive diagnosis of visceral leishmaniasis requires:", "opts": ["A positive rK39 test alone", "Demonstration of the parasite by smear or culture of tissue (usually bone marrow or spleen)", "A negative malaria smear", "Elevated transaminases"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "13-17", "ch": 13, "n": 17, "stem": "Which statement about serology in visceral leishmaniasis is most accurate?", "opts": ["It is definitive proof of active VL in all settings", "It is useful to assess cure because it becomes negative quickly", "It is always negative in immunocompetent patients", "A positive test is not definitive proof of active VL and must be interpreted in clinical and epidemiologic context"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "13-18", "ch": 13, "n": 18, "stem": "In HIV–VL coinfection, which diagnostic statement is most accurate?", "opts": ["Serologic sensitivity is reduced, while high parasite loads increase the sensitivity of blood culture and molecular assays", "Serology is more sensitive than PCR", "VL cannot occur", "Only skin biopsy is useful"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "13-19", "ch": 13, "n": 19, "stem": "Which feature defines \"complicated\" cutaneous leishmaniasis and generally supports systemic therapy?", "opts": ["A single small lesion in an immunocompetent host", "A lesion already healing spontaneously", "Lesions on the face, fingers, toes or genitalia", "Infection acquired outside areas associated with ML risk"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "13-20", "ch": 13, "n": 20, "stem": "Four to six weeks after treatment of CL, the expected response is:", "opts": ["Complete healing of the lesion in all cases", "Lesion size reduced by more than 50%, improving inflammation and re-epithelialization, and no new lesions", "Any worsening always means treatment failure", "Routine parasitologic sampling, even if the lesion is healing"], "a": 1, "sec": ["7"]}, {"id": "14-1", "ch": 14, "n": 1, "stem": "Malaria should be suspected in which scenario?", "opts": ["Afebrile patient with isolated rash and no travel", "Chronic cough with normal vital signs", "Fever (≥37.5°C) with residence in or travel to an endemic area", "Dysuria without systemic symptoms"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "14-2", "ch": 14, "n": 2, "stem": "The typical incubation period for <em>P. falciparum</em> is:", "opts": ["12 to 14 days (range 7 to 30 days)", "1 to 3 days", "2 to 6 months", "2 to 5 years"], "a": 0, "sec": ["4"]}, {"id": "14-3", "ch": 14, "n": 3, "stem": "Relapsing malaria weeks to months after exposure is most associated with:", "opts": ["<em>P. falciparum</em> only", "<em>P. malariae</em> due to cysts", "<em>Babesia</em> due to spores", "<em>P. vivax</em> or <em>P. ovale</em> due to hypnozoites"], "a": 3, "sec": ["1", "4"]}, {"id": "14-4", "ch": 14, "n": 4, "stem": "The standard diagnostic tool for malaria is:", "opts": ["Urine antigen testing", "Light microscopy of stained blood smears", "Chest radiograph", "Stool ova and parasite exam"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "14-5", "ch": 14, "n": 5, "stem": "A key advantage of microscopy over RDTs is:", "opts": ["Species identification and quantification of parasitemia", "Faster results in 5 minutes", "No training required", "Better detection of very low parasitemia than PCR"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "14-6", "ch": 14, "n": 6, "stem": "A key limitation of microscopy is:", "opts": ["It cannot identify species", "It provides only qualitative results", "It requires no expertise", "It cannot reliably detect very low parasitemia (&lt;5 to 10 parasites/µL)"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "14-7", "ch": 14, "n": 7, "stem": "RDTs are especially useful because they:", "opts": ["Quantify parasite density precisely", "Provide results in 15 to 20 minutes and are easy to use", "Require advanced laboratory infrastructure", "Replace microscopy in all settings"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "14-8", "ch": 14, "n": 8, "stem": "RDTs generally cannot:", "opts": ["Detect malaria antigens", "Be used without electricity", "Provide quantitative parasite density", "Be used in resource-limited settings"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "14-9", "ch": 14, "n": 9, "stem": "Reliance on HRP2-based RDTs should be avoided where <em>pfhrp2/3</em> deletions are prevalent, including:", "opts": ["Peruvian Amazon and Eritrea", "Iceland and Greenland", "Japan and Korea", "Canada and the United States"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "14-10", "ch": 14, "n": 10, "stem": "If initial malaria testing is negative but suspicion persists, follow-up testing should be performed:", "opts": ["Once weekly for 4 weeks", "Only if fever exceeds 40°C", "Only after antibiotics fail", "Each day for two more days"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "14-11", "ch": 14, "n": 11, "stem": "Severe malaria is best defined as:", "opts": ["Any malaria with fever", "Malaria with rash", "Acute malaria with major organ dysfunction and/or high parasitemia", "Malaria with isolated thrombocytopenia only"], "a": 2, "sec": ["4.2"]}, {"id": "14-12", "ch": 14, "n": 12, "stem": "Which is a manifestation of severe malaria?", "opts": ["Otitis media", "Hypoglycemia", "Isolated constipation", "Allergic rhinitis"], "a": 1, "sec": ["4.2"]}, {"id": "14-13", "ch": 14, "n": 13, "stem": "Which is a manifestation of severe malaria?", "opts": ["Renal failure and hemoglobinuria (\"blackwater fever\")", "Mild headache only", "Isolated conjunctivitis", "Localized cellulitis"], "a": 0, "sec": ["4.2"]}, {"id": "14-14", "ch": 14, "n": 14, "stem": "Compared with children, adults with severe malaria more often have:", "opts": ["Seizures and severe anemia", "Only neurodevelopmental sequelae", "Only hypoglycemia", "Hyperparasitemia, ARDS and jaundice"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.2"]}, {"id": "14-15", "ch": 14, "n": 15, "stem": "For uncomplicated <em>P. falciparum</em> malaria, the suggested treatment approach is:", "opts": ["Monotherapy with a single artemisinin", "No treatment if afebrile", "Artemisinin combination therapy (ACT)", "Antibiotics alone"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "14-16", "ch": 14, "n": 16, "stem": "If malaria occurs while a patient is taking prophylaxis, treatment should:", "opts": ["Use the same drug as prophylaxis", "Use a different medication for treatment", "Be delayed until repeat smears are positive for 7 days", "Be avoided to prevent resistance"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "14-17", "ch": 14, "n": 17, "stem": "With appropriate therapy for uncomplicated malaria, parasitemia typically clears within:", "opts": ["48 to 72 hours", "6 to 12 hours", "2 to 3 weeks", "2 to 3 months"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.1", "7"]}, {"id": "14-18", "ch": 14, "n": 18, "stem": "Initial treatment of severe malaria consists of:", "opts": ["Oral therapy only", "No antimalarial therapy until species confirmation by PCR", "Antipyretics only", "Parenteral therapy"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "14-19", "ch": 14, "n": 19, "stem": "In areas without artemisinin resistance, the preferred parenteral therapy (when reliable quality is available) is:", "opts": ["IV quinine rather than artesunate", "IV artesunate rather than IV quinine", "Oral ACT only", "Chloroquine only"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "14-20", "ch": 14, "n": 20, "stem": "For travelers at risk of malaria infection, which statement is most accurate?", "opts": ["Chemoprophylaxis is never indicated", "Only mosquito nets matter; repellents do not", "Chemoprophylaxis is recommended, and travelers should seek evaluation for fever during or after travel even if prophylaxis was taken", "Fever after travel excludes malaria if prophylaxis was taken"], "a": 2, "sec": ["8.2"]}, {"id": "15-1", "ch": 15, "n": 1, "stem": "Which herpesviruses establish latency in sensory ganglia?", "opts": ["HSV-1, HSV-2 and VZV", "EBV and HHV-8", "CMV and HHV-6", "HHV-6 and HHV-7"], "a": 0, "sec": ["1.2"]}, {"id": "15-2", "ch": 15, "n": 2, "stem": "Enlarged cells with \"owl's eye\" intranuclear inclusions in a biopsy are typical of:", "opts": ["EBV", "HSV-1", "CMV", "HHV-8"], "a": 2, "sec": ["1.2"]}, {"id": "15-3", "ch": 15, "n": 3, "stem": "Which antiviral drug is the first-line treatment for HSV and VZV infections?", "opts": ["Ganciclovir", "Acyclovir (or valacyclovir)", "Oseltamivir", "Ribavirin"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "15-4", "ch": 15, "n": 4, "stem": "A 58-year-old man has fever, confusion and aphasia. MRI shows left temporal hyperintensity. CSF HSV PCR, taken on day 2 of symptoms, is negative. The best next step is to:", "opts": ["Stop acyclovir, because the PCR is negative", "Give corticosteroids alone", "Order a multiplex CSF PCR panel instead of specific PCR", "Continue IV acyclovir and repeat the lumbar puncture within 3 to 7 days"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.2", "6.2"]}, {"id": "15-5", "ch": 15, "n": 5, "stem": "According to current guidelines, type-specific HSV-2 serology:", "opts": ["Should be used to screen the general population", "Should be confirmed with a second test when the result is low-positive", "Is best performed by detecting IgM", "Is replaced by the Tzanck smear"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "15-6", "ch": 15, "n": 6, "stem": "The risk of neonatal herpes is highest when the mother has:", "opts": ["A primary genital HSV infection near the time of delivery", "A history of recurrent genital herpes and no lesions at delivery", "Recurrent herpes labialis", "HSV-1 IgG antibodies acquired in childhood"], "a": 0, "sec": ["3.2"]}, {"id": "15-7", "ch": 15, "n": 7, "stem": "In a patient with zoster of the first branch of the trigeminal nerve, vesicles on the tip of the nose (Hutchinson sign) indicate:", "opts": ["Bacterial superinfection", "Ramsay Hunt syndrome", "A low risk of complications", "Nasociliary nerve involvement and an increased risk of eye involvement"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.2"]}, {"id": "15-8", "ch": 15, "n": 8, "stem": "Facial palsy with vesicles in the external ear canal suggests VZV reactivation in the:", "opts": ["Trigeminal ganglion", "Thoracic dorsal root ganglia", "Geniculate ganglion", "Sacral ganglia"], "a": 2, "sec": ["4.2"]}, {"id": "15-9", "ch": 15, "n": 9, "stem": "In a patient with herpes zoster, which comorbidity most often requires an adjustment of antiviral therapy with acyclovir or valacyclovir?", "opts": ["Chronic kidney disease", "Hypertension", "Hypothyroidism", "Osteoarthritis"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "15-10", "ch": 15, "n": 10, "stem": "A newborn is at highest risk of severe neonatal varicella when the mother's varicella rash begins:", "opts": ["In the first trimester", "At 20 weeks of gestation", "One month before delivery", "From 5 days before to 2 days after delivery"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.2", "8.2"]}, {"id": "15-11", "ch": 15, "n": 11, "stem": "A false-negative heterophile antibody test in EBV mononucleosis is most likely:", "opts": ["In adolescents in the third week of illness", "In adults with splenomegaly", "In children under 4 years and early in the illness", "In patients with atypical lymphocytosis"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "15-12", "ch": 15, "n": 12, "stem": "EBV serology shows VCA IgM positive, VCA IgG positive, EBNA IgG negative. This indicates:", "opts": ["No previous EBV infection", "Acute primary EBV infection", "Past EBV infection", "Chronic active EBV disease, by definition"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "15-13", "ch": 15, "n": 13, "stem": "A 19-year-old with mononucleosis treated with amoxicillin develops a maculopapular rash. The correct statement is:", "opts": ["The patient has a confirmed lifelong penicillin allergy", "The rash proves bacterial co-infection", "Acyclovir is indicated", "This rash is frequent in mononucleosis and does not usually mean a true penicillin allergy"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.3"]}, {"id": "15-14", "ch": 15, "n": 14, "stem": "In solid organ transplantation, the highest risk of CMV disease is in:", "opts": ["Donor seropositive, recipient seronegative (D+/R−)", "Donor seronegative, recipient seronegative (D−/R−)", "Donor seronegative, recipient seropositive (D−/R+)", "Risk does not depend on serostatus"], "a": 0, "sec": ["2.2"]}, {"id": "15-15", "ch": 15, "n": 15, "stem": "Which drug is the first-line treatment of CMV disease in transplant recipients?", "opts": ["Acyclovir", "Ganciclovir (IV) or valganciclovir (oral)", "Oseltamivir", "Fluconazole"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.5"]}, {"id": "15-16", "ch": 15, "n": 16, "stem": "The main adverse effect to monitor during treatment with ganciclovir or valganciclovir is:", "opts": ["Hepatotoxicity", "QT prolongation", "Neutropenia (bone marrow suppression)", "Ototoxicity"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "15-17", "ch": 15, "n": 17, "stem": "Congenital CMV infection in a newborn is best diagnosed by:", "opts": ["CMV PCR on urine or saliva within the first 21 days of life", "CMV IgG in the newborn's serum", "CMV IgG avidity in the newborn", "CMV PCR on urine at 3 months of age"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "15-18", "ch": 15, "n": 18, "stem": "HHV-8 is associated with:", "opts": ["Burkitt lymphoma and nasopharyngeal carcinoma", "Exanthema subitum", "Pityriasis rosea", "Kaposi sarcoma, primary effusion lymphoma and multicentric Castleman disease"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.5"]}, {"id": "15-19", "ch": 15, "n": 19, "stem": "Exanthema subitum (roseola infantum) is characterized by:", "opts": ["A vesicular rash in different stages of evolution", "High fever for 3 to 5 days, then a rash as the fever falls", "A \"slapped cheek\" rash", "Rash and fever starting together, with conjunctivitis and Koplik spots"], "a": 1, "sec": ["4.5"]}, {"id": "15-20", "ch": 15, "n": 20, "stem": "The recombinant zoster vaccine:", "opts": ["Is a live attenuated vaccine contraindicated in immunocompromised patients", "Is a single dose given at age 50", "Is a non-live adjuvanted glycoprotein E vaccine, given in two doses, usable in immunocompromised adults", "Must not be given to people who have already had zoster"], "a": 2, "sec": ["8.1"]}, {"id": "16-1", "ch": 16, "n": 1, "stem": "Which hepatitis virus has a DNA genome?", "opts": ["HAV", "HBV", "HCV", "HEV"], "a": 1, "sec": ["1.1"]}, {"id": "16-2", "ch": 16, "n": 2, "stem": "HDV can only infect hepatocytes in the presence of HBV because it needs:", "opts": ["The HAV capsid", "The HCV core protein", "The HBV NS5A protein", "The HBV envelope protein (HBsAg)"], "a": 3, "sec": ["1.3"]}, {"id": "16-3", "ch": 16, "n": 3, "stem": "The risk of developing chronic HBV infection is highest when infection is acquired:", "opts": ["Perinatally", "In adolescence", "In adulthood through sexual contact", "In adulthood through a needlestick injury"], "a": 0, "sec": ["2.3"]}, {"id": "16-4", "ch": 16, "n": 4, "stem": "In Europe, the main source of autochthonous HEV genotype 3 infection is:", "opts": ["Contaminated drinking water after monsoon floods", "Sexual transmission", "Undercooked pork, wild boar or game meat", "Mosquito bites"], "a": 2, "sec": ["2.2"]}, {"id": "16-5", "ch": 16, "n": 5, "stem": "Acute HEV genotype 1 infection carries the highest risk of death in:", "opts": ["Children under 5 years", "Healthy young men", "Pregnant women in the third trimester", "Vaccinated travelers"], "a": 2, "sec": ["3.6"]}, {"id": "16-6", "ch": 16, "n": 6, "stem": "After acute HCV infection, the proportion of adults who develop chronic infection is:", "opts": ["The majority (roughly 70–75%)", "Less than 5%", "About 10%", "100%"], "a": 0, "sec": ["3.5"]}, {"id": "16-7", "ch": 16, "n": 7, "stem": "A patient has HBsAg positive, anti-HBc IgM positive and anti-HBs negative. The most likely diagnosis is:", "opts": ["Chronic HBV infection in the HBeAg-negative phase", "Immunity from vaccination", "Resolved past infection", "Acute hepatitis B"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "16-8", "ch": 16, "n": 8, "stem": "A healthcare student has anti-HBs positive, with HBsAg and anti-HBc negative. This profile indicates:", "opts": ["Acute HBV infection", "Immunity from vaccination", "Occult HBV infection", "Window period of acute HBV"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "16-9", "ch": 16, "n": 9, "stem": "A patient is anti-HCV positive and HCV RNA negative on two occasions. The most likely interpretation is:", "opts": ["Resolved HCV infection (or false-positive antibody)", "Chronic active hepatitis C", "Acute HCV in the window period", "Protective immunity against reinfection"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.4"]}, {"id": "16-10", "ch": 16, "n": 10, "stem": "Acute hepatitis A is diagnosed by:", "opts": ["Anti-HAV IgG", "Liver biopsy", "Anti-HAV IgM", "HAV genotyping"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "16-11", "ch": 16, "n": 11, "stem": "A kidney transplant recipient has persistently raised ALT. The best test to diagnose chronic hepatitis E is:", "opts": ["Anti-HEV IgM alone", "HEV RNA by nucleic acid testing", "Anti-HEV IgG alone", "Repeat ALT in 6 months"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.6"]}, {"id": "16-12", "ch": 16, "n": 12, "stem": "First-line nucleos(t)ide analogues for chronic hepatitis B are:", "opts": ["Lamivudine", "Adefovir", "Telbivudine", "Entecavir, tenofovir disoproxil or tenofovir alafenamide"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "16-13", "ch": 16, "n": 13, "stem": "Which statement about current treatment of chronic hepatitis C is correct?", "opts": ["Interferon remains part of first-line therapy", "Treatment must be continued for life to keep the virus suppressed", "Treatment is reserved for patients with cirrhosis", "Short courses (8 to 12 weeks) of oral DAAs cure more than 95% of patients"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.4"]}, {"id": "16-14", "ch": 16, "n": 14, "stem": "Cure of hepatitis C is defined as:", "opts": ["Undetectable HCV RNA 12 weeks after the end of treatment (SVR12)", "Disappearance of anti-HCV antibodies", "Normal ALT at the end of treatment", "Undetectable HCV RNA at week 4 of treatment"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.4"]}, {"id": "16-15", "ch": 16, "n": 15, "stem": "Which patients should be tested for hepatitis D (anti-HDV)?", "opts": ["Only patients with acute hepatitis B", "Only patients with cirrhosis", "All HBsAg-positive patients", "Only people who inject drugs"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.5"]}, {"id": "16-16", "ch": 16, "n": 16, "stem": "A patient who is HBsAg negative and anti-HBc positive is about to start rituximab. The correct approach is:", "opts": ["No action, because HBsAg is negative", "Antiviral prophylaxis with entecavir or tenofovir", "HBV vaccination only", "Pegylated interferon for 48 weeks"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "16-17", "ch": 16, "n": 17, "stem": "After successful treatment of hepatitis C (SVR12), which statement is correct?", "opts": ["Anti-HCV antibodies become negative", "The patient is protected against reinfection", "Patients with cirrhosis remain at risk of liver cancer and need continued surveillance", "Antiviral therapy must be continued for life"], "a": 2, "sec": ["7.3"]}, {"id": "16-18", "ch": 16, "n": 18, "stem": "A newborn of an HBsAg-positive mother should receive:", "opts": ["HBV vaccine at 2 months only", "HBIG alone, without vaccine", "Tenofovir for 6 months", "HBV vaccine plus HBIG within 12–24 hours of birth"], "a": 3, "sec": ["8.2"]}, {"id": "16-19", "ch": 16, "n": 19, "stem": "A healthy 30-year-old was exposed to a household contact with acute hepatitis A 5 days ago. The recommended post-exposure prophylaxis is:", "opts": ["One dose of hepatitis A vaccine", "Ribavirin", "No prophylaxis", "Hepatitis B immune globulin"], "a": 0, "sec": ["8.1"]}, {"id": "16-20", "ch": 16, "n": 20, "stem": "For which hepatitis virus is there no vaccine available?", "opts": ["HAV", "HCV", "HBV", "HEV in China"], "a": 1, "sec": ["8.3"]}, {"id": "17-1", "ch": 17, "n": 1, "stem": "Which HIV enzyme converts the viral RNA genome into DNA?", "opts": ["Reverse transcriptase", "Integrase", "Protease", "RNA-dependent RNA polymerase"], "a": 0, "sec": ["1.1–1.2"]}, {"id": "17-2", "ch": 17, "n": 2, "stem": "People homozygous for the CCR5-Δ32 deletion are largely resistant to infection with:", "opts": ["Hepatitis B virus", "CXCR4-tropic HIV-1", "CCR5-tropic HIV-1", "All retroviruses"], "a": 2, "sec": ["3.1"]}, {"id": "17-3", "ch": 17, "n": 3, "stem": "Without treatment, the median time from HIV infection to AIDS is about:", "opts": ["6 months", "10 years", "2 years", "25 years"], "a": 1, "sec": ["3.2"]}, {"id": "17-4", "ch": 17, "n": 4, "stem": "Which route carries the highest per-act risk of HIV transmission?", "opts": ["Receptive vaginal intercourse", "Occupational needlestick injury", "Insertive anal intercourse", "Receptive anal intercourse"], "a": 3, "sec": ["2.3"]}, {"id": "17-5", "ch": 17, "n": 5, "stem": "In Italy, about what proportion of new HIV diagnoses had a CD4 count below 350 cells/µL?", "opts": ["About 10%", "About 25%", "About 60%", "About 90%"], "a": 2, "sec": ["2.2"]}, {"id": "17-6", "ch": 17, "n": 6, "stem": "Massive CD4+ T-cell depletion during acute HIV infection occurs mainly in:", "opts": ["Gut-associated lymphoid tissue", "Bone marrow", "Thymus", "Peripheral blood only"], "a": 0, "sec": ["3.2"]}, {"id": "17-7", "ch": 17, "n": 7, "stem": "ART must be continued for life mainly because:", "opts": ["CD4 counts never recover", "All ART drugs are virucidal only at high doses", "Resistance to all drug classes develops within one year", "Integrated provirus in resting memory CD4+ T cells is not eliminated by ART"], "a": 3, "sec": ["3.4"]}, {"id": "17-8", "ch": 17, "n": 8, "stem": "A 24-year-old man has fever, pharyngitis, a maculopapular rash, oral ulcers and lymphadenopathy two weeks after condomless sex. A rapid antibody test is negative. The best next step is:", "opts": ["Reassure him; the negative test excludes HIV", "Order a 4th-generation Ag/Ab assay and HIV-1 RNA", "Repeat the antibody test in 6 months", "Order a Western blot"], "a": 1, "sec": ["4.1", "5.2"]}, {"id": "17-9", "ch": 17, "n": 9, "stem": "In the recommended laboratory testing algorithm, a reactive 4th-generation Ag/Ab assay followed by a negative antibody differentiation assay should be followed by:", "opts": ["A Western blot", "HIV-1 RNA (nucleic acid test)", "A CD4 count", "A repeat screening test in 1 year"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "17-10", "ch": 17, "n": 10, "stem": "Which test is NOT part of the baseline evaluation of a patient with newly diagnosed HIV?", "opts": ["CD4 count", "HIV RNA (viral load)", "Routine brain MRI", "Hepatitis B and C serology"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.4"]}, {"id": "17-11", "ch": 17, "n": 11, "stem": "A patient with HIV has a 3-week history of dry cough, exertional dyspnea, desaturation on exertion, raised LDH and bilateral ground-glass infiltrates. The CD4 count is 90 cells/µL. The most likely diagnosis is:", "opts": ["<em>Pneumocystis jirovecii</em> pneumonia", "Pneumococcal lobar pneumonia", "Kaposi sarcoma of the lung", "CMV pneumonitis"], "a": 0, "sec": ["4.3"]}, {"id": "17-12", "ch": 17, "n": 12, "stem": "Which opportunistic infection typically occurs at CD4 counts below 50 cells/µL?", "opts": ["Oral candidiasis", "Herpes zoster", "Pulmonary tuberculosis", "CMV retinitis"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.3"]}, {"id": "17-13", "ch": 17, "n": 13, "stem": "According to current guidelines, ART should be started:", "opts": ["Only when CD4 falls below 350 cells/µL", "Only in patients with an AIDS-defining illness", "Only after resistance test results are available", "In all people with HIV, as soon as possible, regardless of CD4 count"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "17-14", "ch": 17, "n": 14, "stem": "In a patient with newly diagnosed HIV and cryptococcal meningitis, ART should be:", "opts": ["Started on the same day as antifungal therapy", "Deferred for about 4 to 6 weeks after starting antifungal therapy", "Deferred until the CD4 count exceeds 500 cells/µL", "Never started"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "17-15", "ch": 17, "n": 15, "stem": "In a patient with HIV and chronic hepatitis B, which regimen is NOT appropriate?", "opts": ["Dolutegravir/lamivudine (two-drug regimen)", "Bictegravir/TAF/FTC", "Dolutegravir + TDF + FTC", "Dolutegravir + TAF + FTC"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "17-16", "ch": 17, "n": 16, "stem": "For most people starting ART, the preferred regimen is:", "opts": ["A single protease inhibitor", "Two NRTIs without a third drug", "An integrase inhibitor plus one or two NRTIs", "A single NNRTI"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "17-17", "ch": 17, "n": 17, "stem": "Three weeks after starting ART, a patient treated for TB develops new fever and enlarging lymph nodes while the viral load is falling. The most likely explanation is:", "opts": ["Virological failure", "Drug-resistant HIV", "Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome", "Acute HIV reinfection"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.4"]}, {"id": "17-18", "ch": 17, "n": 18, "stem": "Primary prophylaxis with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is indicated when the CD4 count is below:", "opts": ["500 cells/µL", "350 cells/µL", "50 cells/µL", "200 cells/µL"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.5"]}, {"id": "17-19", "ch": 17, "n": 19, "stem": "Post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) for HIV should be started:", "opts": ["As soon as possible and no later than 72 hours after exposure, for 28 days", "Within 7 days of exposure, for 14 days", "Only after the source's HIV test results are available", "Within 72 hours, for 3 months"], "a": 0, "sec": ["8.3"]}, {"id": "17-20", "ch": 17, "n": 20, "stem": "Which statement about HIV prevention is correct?", "opts": ["A licensed HIV vaccine is available for high-risk groups", "A person with a durably undetectable viral load does not transmit HIV sexually", "Live vaccines are safe at any CD4 count", "On-demand oral PrEP is recommended for vaginal exposure"], "a": 1, "sec": ["8.1"]}, {"id": "18-1", "ch": 18, "n": 1, "stem": "A 28-year-old man has a single painless, indurated ulcer on the penis with firm, non-tender inguinal nodes. The most likely cause is:", "opts": ["Genital herpes", "Primary syphilis", "Chancroid", "Mpox"], "a": 1, "sec": ["4.2", "4.7"]}, {"id": "18-2", "ch": 18, "n": 2, "stem": "Which organism lacks a cell wall and is therefore intrinsically resistant to β-lactams?", "opts": ["<em>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</em>", "<em>Treponema pallidum</em>", "<em>Chlamydia trachomatis</em>", "<em>Mycoplasma genitalium</em>"], "a": 3, "sec": ["1"]}, {"id": "18-3", "ch": 18, "n": 3, "stem": "The most common curable STI worldwide is:", "opts": ["Trichomoniasis", "Chlamydia", "Gonorrhea", "Syphilis"], "a": 0, "sec": ["2"]}, {"id": "18-4", "ch": 18, "n": 4, "stem": "A rash involving the palms and soles, mucous patches and generalized lymphadenopathy suggest:", "opts": ["Primary syphilis", "Late latent syphilis", "Secondary syphilis", "Tertiary syphilis"], "a": 2, "sec": ["4.2"]}, {"id": "18-5", "ch": 18, "n": 5, "stem": "Which statement about neurosyphilis is correct?", "opts": ["It occurs only decades after infection", "A negative CSF-VDRL excludes it", "It can occur at any stage of syphilis", "It is treated with a single dose of benzathine penicillin"], "a": 2, "sec": ["3.1", "5.4"]}, {"id": "18-6", "ch": 18, "n": 6, "stem": "The diagnostic test of choice for chlamydia and gonorrhea is:", "opts": ["Nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT)", "Serology", "Gram stain of a vaginal swab", "Blood culture"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "18-7", "ch": 18, "n": 7, "stem": "An MSM on HIV PrEP comes for routine screening and has no symptoms. Which approach is correct?", "opts": ["Urine NAAT only", "No testing because he is asymptomatic", "Syphilis serology only", "NAAT at genital, rectal and pharyngeal sites, plus syphilis and HIV serology"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "18-8", "ch": 18, "n": 8, "stem": "Which test is used to monitor the response to syphilis treatment?", "opts": ["TPPA", "Quantitative RPR or VDRL", "FTA-ABS", "Treponemal EIA"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.3", "7.2"]}, {"id": "18-9", "ch": 18, "n": 9, "stem": "A woman with secondary syphilis and very high antibody titers has a negative undiluted RPR. The most likely explanation is:", "opts": ["Prozone effect", "Cured infection", "False-positive treponemal test", "HIV co-infection invalidating serology"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.3"]}, {"id": "18-10", "ch": 18, "n": 10, "stem": "Which syphilis presentation is treated as neurosyphilis?", "opts": ["Primary chancre", "Early latent syphilis", "Ocular syphilis", "Condylomata lata"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.4"]}, {"id": "18-11", "ch": 18, "n": 11, "stem": "The recommended treatment for uncomplicated gonorrhea is based on:", "opts": ["Ciprofloxacin", "Ceftriaxone", "Doxycycline alone", "Amoxicillin"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "18-12", "ch": 18, "n": 12, "stem": "For uncomplicated urogenital and rectal chlamydia in a non-pregnant adult, the preferred treatment is:", "opts": ["Single-dose azithromycin", "Ceftriaxone", "Metronidazole", "Doxycycline for 7 days"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "18-13", "ch": 18, "n": 13, "stem": "A pregnant woman with early syphilis reports a penicillin allergy. The correct management is:", "opts": ["Azithromycin", "Doxycycline", "No treatment until delivery", "Penicillin desensitization, then penicillin"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.4"]}, {"id": "18-14", "ch": 18, "n": 14, "stem": "Fever, headache and myalgia appear 6 hours after penicillin for secondary syphilis. This is:", "opts": ["Penicillin allergy, so switch drug", "Jarisch–Herxheimer reaction", "Treatment failure", "Neurosyphilis"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.4"]}, {"id": "18-15", "ch": 18, "n": 15, "stem": "An MSM has proctitis with bloody discharge and a positive rectal chlamydia NAAT. The most appropriate treatment is:", "opts": ["Single-dose azithromycin", "Ceftriaxone alone", "Doxycycline for 21 days (lymphogranuloma venereum)", "Metronidazole"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.3"]}, {"id": "18-16", "ch": 18, "n": 16, "stem": "Treatment of <em>M. genitalium</em> should be guided by:", "opts": ["Macrolide-resistance mutation testing", "Gram stain", "Culture on routine media", "Serology"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.2", "6.5"]}, {"id": "18-17", "ch": 18, "n": 17, "stem": "After treatment of early syphilis, an adequate serologic response is:", "opts": ["A negative treponemal test at 6 months", "Any decline in RPR titer", "A fourfold (two-dilution) decline in RPR titer within 6–12 months", "A stable RPR titer"], "a": 2, "sec": ["7.2"]}, {"id": "18-18", "ch": 18, "n": 18, "stem": "A test of cure for gonorrhea is recommended routinely for:", "opts": ["Urethral infection in men", "Cervical infection", "Rectal infection", "Pharyngeal infection"], "a": 3, "sec": ["7.1"]}, {"id": "18-19", "ch": 18, "n": 19, "stem": "Doxy-PEP should be offered to:", "opts": ["MSM and transgender women with a bacterial STI in the past 12 months", "All sexually active adults", "Cisgender women using contraception", "Anyone starting HIV PrEP, whatever their STI history"], "a": 0, "sec": ["8.1"]}, {"id": "18-20", "ch": 18, "n": 20, "stem": "Doxycycline post-exposure prophylaxis (doxy-PEP) is least effective against:", "opts": ["Chlamydia", "Gonorrhea", "Syphilis", "It is equally effective against all three"], "a": 1, "sec": ["8.1"]}, {"id": "19-1", "ch": 19, "n": 1, "stem": "Which finding in tissue is most typical of <em>Aspergillus</em>?", "opts": ["Broad, ribbon-like, pauci-septate hyphae branching at right angles", "Narrow, regularly septate hyphae with acute-angle (about 45°) branching", "Encapsulated budding yeasts stained by India ink", "Spherules containing endospores"], "a": 1, "sec": ["1.1"]}, {"id": "19-2", "ch": 19, "n": 2, "stem": "How is <em>Aspergillus</em> infection usually acquired?", "opts": ["Person-to-person contact", "Ingestion of contaminated food", "Through intravascular catheters", "Inhalation of airborne conidia from the environment"], "a": 3, "sec": ["1.1"]}, {"id": "19-3", "ch": 19, "n": 3, "stem": "Which patients are at highest risk of invasive pulmonary aspergillosis?", "opts": ["Patients with prolonged, profound neutropenia (e.g. acute leukemia on chemotherapy)", "Healthy young adults after a viral upper respiratory infection", "Pregnant women", "Patients with well-controlled type 2 diabetes"], "a": 0, "sec": ["2.1"]}, {"id": "19-4", "ch": 19, "n": 4, "stem": "Which host defense cell is mainly responsible for killing <em>Aspergillus</em> hyphae?", "opts": ["Eosinophil", "B lymphocyte", "Neutrophil", "Mast cell"], "a": 2, "sec": ["3.1"]}, {"id": "19-5", "ch": 19, "n": 5, "stem": "A patient with AML has been neutropenic for 14 days and is febrile despite 5 days of broad-spectrum antibiotics. Chest CT shows a nodule surrounded by ground-glass opacity. This is:", "opts": ["The air crescent sign, typical of neutrophil recovery", "The reversed halo sign, typical of IPA", "The halo sign, an early sign of angioinvasive fungal infection", "A finding that excludes a fungal infection"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "19-6", "ch": 19, "n": 6, "stem": "Which statement about galactomannan is correct?", "opts": ["BAL galactomannan is more sensitive than serum galactomannan, especially in non-neutropenic patients", "It is a marker specific for <em>Candida</em> species", "Its sensitivity increases during mould-active prophylaxis", "It is part of the cell wall of Mucorales"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "19-7", "ch": 19, "n": 7, "stem": "According to current consensus definitions, a patient with a host factor and a typical CT lesion, but no mycological evidence, has:", "opts": ["Proven IPA", "Probable IPA", "Excluded IPA", "Possible IPA"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.1"]}, {"id": "19-8", "ch": 19, "n": 8, "stem": "Which is a recommended first-line drug for invasive pulmonary aspergillosis?", "opts": ["Fluconazole", "Isavuconazole", "Caspofungin monotherapy", "Flucytosine"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "19-9", "ch": 19, "n": 9, "stem": "Therapeutic drug monitoring is especially recommended for voriconazole because:", "opts": ["Its pharmacokinetics are non-linear and highly variable (e.g. CYP2C19 polymorphisms)", "It is excreted unchanged in urine", "It has no adverse effects at any concentration", "It is only available as an inhaled formulation"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "19-10", "ch": 19, "n": 10, "stem": "Which tests are used to follow the response to treatment of invasive pulmonary aspergillosis?", "opts": ["Procalcitonin and C-reactive protein", "Repeated blood cultures", "Chest CT and serum galactomannan", "Sputum Gram stain"], "a": 2, "sec": ["7.1"]}, {"id": "19-11", "ch": 19, "n": 11, "stem": "Which risk factor is most typical of <em>Candida parapsilosis</em> bloodstream infection?", "opts": ["Inhalation of spores during construction work", "Chronic granulomatous disease", "Asthma with ABPA", "Central venous catheter and parenteral nutrition, with transmission via healthcare workers' hands"], "a": 3, "sec": ["1.2", "3.2"]}, {"id": "19-12", "ch": 19, "n": 12, "stem": "Which <em>Candida</em> species is intrinsically resistant to fluconazole?", "opts": ["<em>C. albicans</em>", "<em>C. krusei</em> (<em>Pichia kudriavzevii</em>)", "<em>C. parapsilosis</em>", "<em>C. tropicalis</em>"], "a": 1, "sec": ["1.2"]}, {"id": "19-13", "ch": 19, "n": 13, "stem": "The sensitivity of blood cultures for all forms of invasive candidiasis (including deep-seated infection) is approximately:", "opts": ["100%", "50%", "95%", "5%"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "19-14", "ch": 19, "n": 14, "stem": "Which statement about β-D-glucan is correct?", "opts": ["It is specific for <em>Aspergillus</em>", "It is never falsely positive", "It is a pan-fungal marker with a high negative predictive value", "It detects Mucorales reliably"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.1", "5.2"]}, {"id": "19-15", "ch": 19, "n": 15, "stem": "The first-line treatment for candidemia in a critically ill, non-neutropenic patient is:", "opts": ["An echinocandin", "Fluconazole in all cases", "Voriconazole monotherapy", "No therapy until species identification"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "19-16", "ch": 19, "n": 16, "stem": "Step-down from an echinocandin to oral fluconazole in candidemia is appropriate when:", "opts": ["The first blood culture becomes positive", "The isolate is <em>C. krusei</em>", "The patient is still in septic shock", "The patient is clinically stable, the isolate is fluconazole-susceptible and repeat blood cultures are negative"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "19-17", "ch": 19, "n": 17, "stem": "The usual duration of therapy for uncomplicated candidemia is:", "opts": ["3 days after the first positive blood culture", "7 days after catheter removal", "6 weeks in all patients", "14 days after the first negative blood culture and resolution of symptoms"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "19-18", "ch": 19, "n": 18, "stem": "Which test should be performed in every patient with candidemia?", "opts": ["Dilated fundoscopy", "Bone marrow biopsy", "Lumbar puncture", "Bronchoscopy"], "a": 0, "sec": ["7.2"]}, {"id": "19-19", "ch": 19, "n": 19, "stem": "In a patient with candidemia whose central venous catheter is the likely source, the correct approach is to:", "opts": ["Leave the catheter in place and treat through it", "Treat with antifungals only if fever persists", "Remove the catheter in addition to antifungal therapy", "Change the catheter over a guidewire without antifungal therapy"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "19-20", "ch": 19, "n": 20, "stem": "Posaconazole prophylaxis is indicated mainly in which population?", "opts": ["All ICU patients on mechanical ventilation", "Patients with AML/MDS during induction chemotherapy and allogeneic HSCT recipients with GVHD", "Healthy travelers to the tropics", "Patients with asymptomatic candiduria"], "a": 1, "sec": ["8.1"]}, {"id": "20-1", "ch": 20, "n": 1, "stem": "Which tick is the main vector of Lyme borreliosis and tick-borne encephalitis in Europe?", "opts": ["<em>Rhipicephalus sanguineus</em>", "<em>Ixodes ricinus</em>", "<em>Hyalomma marginatum</em>", "<em>Ornithodoros erraticus</em>"], "a": 1, "sec": ["1.1"]}, {"id": "20-2", "ch": 20, "n": 2, "stem": "Acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans is mainly associated with which <em>Borrelia</em> genospecies?", "opts": ["<em>B. garinii</em>", "<em>B. burgdorferi</em> sensu stricto", "<em>B. miyamotoi</em>", "<em>B. afzelii</em>"], "a": 3, "sec": ["1.2", "4.1"]}, {"id": "20-3", "ch": 20, "n": 3, "stem": "Mediterranean spotted fever is transmitted mainly by:", "opts": ["The brown dog tick, <em>Rhipicephalus sanguineus</em>", "The lone star tick, <em>Amblyomma americanum</em>", "Body lice", "Soft ticks of the genus <em>Ornithodoros</em>"], "a": 0, "sec": ["1.1", "2.2"]}, {"id": "20-4", "ch": 20, "n": 4, "stem": "Besides tick bites, tick-borne encephalitis can be transmitted by:", "opts": ["Respiratory droplets from infected patients", "Mosquito bites", "Unpasteurised milk from infected goats, sheep or cows", "Contaminated drinking water"], "a": 2, "sec": ["2.7"]}, {"id": "20-5", "ch": 20, "n": 5, "stem": "A 35-year-old hiker presents with an expanding, non-itchy erythematous patch of 8 cm on the thigh with central clearing, 10 days after a walk in the woods in Trentino. What is the correct next step?", "opts": ["Start oral doxycycline without serological testing", "Request two-tier Lyme serology and wait for the result", "Perform a skin biopsy for culture", "Perform a lumbar puncture"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.2", "6.1"]}, {"id": "20-6", "ch": 20, "n": 6, "stem": "Bannwarth syndrome, typical of European neuroborreliosis, consists of:", "opts": ["Chronic progressive encephalomyelitis with spastic paraparesis", "Acute flaccid paralysis of both legs", "Painful meningoradiculitis, often with facial palsy and lymphocytic CSF", "Bilateral optic neuritis"], "a": 2, "sec": ["4.1"]}, {"id": "20-7", "ch": 20, "n": 7, "stem": "Which statement about Lyme carditis is correct?", "opts": ["It usually requires early implantation of a permanent pacemaker", "It typically causes AV block that can fluctuate rapidly and usually resolves with antibiotics", "It is the most common manifestation of late Lyme disease", "It is treated with prolonged antibiotic courses of at least 3 months"], "a": 1, "sec": ["4.1", "6.1"]}, {"id": "20-8", "ch": 20, "n": 8, "stem": "Which laboratory pattern is most typical of human granulocytic anaplasmosis?", "opts": ["Neutrophilia, eosinophilia and normal transaminases", "Haemolytic anaemia with schistocytes", "Lymphocytosis with atypical lymphocytes", "Leukopenia, thrombocytopenia and raised transaminases"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.3"]}, {"id": "20-9", "ch": 20, "n": 9, "stem": "A 5-year-old boy from Sicily has high fever, a maculopapular rash on the trunk, palms and soles, and a black eschar behind the ear. Which treatment is first-line?", "opts": ["Doxycycline", "Amoxicillin-clavulanate", "Ciprofloxacin", "Ceftriaxone"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.2"]}, {"id": "20-10", "ch": 20, "n": 10, "stem": "Which finding on a blood smear is pathognomonic of babesiosis?", "opts": ["Banana-shaped gametocytes", "Schüffner's dots", "Tetrads of merozoites forming a \"Maltese cross\"", "Spirochetes between red blood cells"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.6"]}, {"id": "20-11", "ch": 20, "n": 11, "stem": "The preferred treatment for babesiosis is:", "opts": ["Chloroquine", "Atovaquone + azithromycin", "Artesunate", "Doxycycline"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.5"]}, {"id": "20-12", "ch": 20, "n": 12, "stem": "In a traveller from East Africa with recurrent fever episodes every week, the best diagnostic test is:", "opts": ["Lyme two-tier serology", "Weil-Felix test", "Stool culture", "Blood smear collected during a febrile episode"], "a": 3, "sec": ["5.7"]}, {"id": "20-13", "ch": 20, "n": 13, "stem": "Shortly after the first dose of doxycycline for tick-borne relapsing fever, the patient develops rigors, high fever and hypotension. This is most likely:", "opts": ["A Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction", "An anaphylactic reaction to doxycycline", "Treatment failure due to resistance", "A co-infection with TBE virus"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.6"]}, {"id": "20-14", "ch": 20, "n": 14, "stem": "A hunter with pneumonic tularemia and signs of sepsis should be treated with:", "opts": ["Amoxicillin", "Azithromycin", "Gentamicin", "Ceftriaxone"], "a": 2, "sec": ["6.4"]}, {"id": "20-15", "ch": 20, "n": 15, "stem": "The typical course of tick-borne encephalitis caused by the European subtype is:", "opts": ["Monophasic haemorrhagic fever", "Biphasic: flu-like viraemic phase, asymptomatic interval, then neurological phase", "Chronic progressive dementia", "Recurrent fever every week for several months"], "a": 1, "sec": ["4.7"]}, {"id": "20-16", "ch": 20, "n": 16, "stem": "Which statement about Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever is correct?", "opts": ["It is transmitted only by mosquito bites", "Infected livestock usually develop severe haemorrhagic disease", "Ribavirin has been proven to reduce mortality in randomised trials", "Person-to-person transmission through blood and body fluids, including in hospitals, can occur"], "a": 3, "sec": ["2.8", "6.8"]}, {"id": "20-17", "ch": 20, "n": 17, "stem": "A patient treated correctly for Lyme disease 1 year ago complains of persistent fatigue and diffuse pain. Serology is still IgG-positive. What is correct?", "opts": ["Further prolonged antibiotic therapy is not recommended because trials showed no benefit", "A 3-month course of IV ceftriaxone is indicated", "Persistent IgG proves active infection", "CD57 counts should be used to guide treatment"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "20-18", "ch": 20, "n": 18, "stem": "Single-dose doxycycline after a tick bite is recommended when:", "opts": ["Any tick bite occurs, regardless of the tick species", "The tick tests positive for <em>Borrelia</em> by PCR", "An <em>Ixodes</em> tick was attached for ≥36 hours in a highly endemic area and prophylaxis can start within 72 hours of removal", "Lyme serology is positive the day after the bite"], "a": 2, "sec": ["8.2"]}, {"id": "20-19", "ch": 20, "n": 19, "stem": "Which statement about TBE vaccines used in Europe is correct?", "opts": ["They are live attenuated vaccines contraindicated in all adults", "They are inactivated vaccines given as a 3-dose primary series followed by boosters", "A single dose gives lifelong protection", "They protect only against the Far Eastern subtype"], "a": 1, "sec": ["8.4"]}, {"id": "20-20", "ch": 20, "n": 20, "stem": "What is the correct way to remove an attached tick?", "opts": ["Cover it with petroleum jelly and wait until it detaches", "Burn it with a match", "Crush it with bare fingers and twist it out", "Grasp it close to the skin with fine-tipped tweezers and pull upwards with steady pressure"], "a": 3, "sec": ["8.1"]}, {"id": "21-1", "ch": 21, "n": 1, "stem": "Which of the following viruses is NOT a flavivirus?", "opts": ["Dengue virus", "West Nile virus", "Chikungunya virus", "Yellow fever virus"], "a": 2, "sec": ["1.1"]}, {"id": "21-2", "ch": 21, "n": 2, "stem": "In Italy, the main vector of West Nile virus is:", "opts": ["<em>Culex pipiens</em>", "<em>Aedes albopictus</em>", "<em>Phlebotomus perniciosus</em>", "<em>Culicoides paraensis</em>"], "a": 0, "sec": ["1.2"]}, {"id": "21-3", "ch": 21, "n": 3, "stem": "Toscana virus is transmitted to humans by:", "opts": ["<em>Aedes albopictus</em>", "Hard ticks", "<em>Culex</em> mosquitoes", "Sandflies"], "a": 3, "sec": ["1.2"]}, {"id": "21-4", "ch": 21, "n": 4, "stem": "Which mechanism best explains why a second dengue infection with a different serotype carries a higher risk of severe disease?", "opts": ["Direct destruction of hepatocytes by cytotoxic T cells", "Antibody-dependent enhancement with increased viremia and endothelial dysfunction", "Integration of the viral genome into host cells", "Production of a bacterial-like exotoxin by the virus"], "a": 1, "sec": ["3.2"]}, {"id": "21-5", "ch": 21, "n": 5, "stem": "In dengue, the critical phase with plasma leakage typically occurs:", "opts": ["In the first 24 hours of fever", "Around defervescence, usually between days 3 and 7", "Three weeks after the onset of fever", "Only in primary infections"], "a": 1, "sec": ["4.2"]}, {"id": "21-6", "ch": 21, "n": 6, "stem": "Which of the following is a warning sign in dengue?", "opts": ["Retro-orbital pain", "Maculopapular rash", "Myalgia", "Persistent vomiting"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.2"]}, {"id": "21-7", "ch": 21, "n": 7, "stem": "Which clinical feature most helps distinguish chikungunya from dengue?", "opts": ["Severe, disabling, symmetrical polyarthralgia", "Marked thrombocytopenia with bleeding", "Plasma leakage with shock", "Jaundice with relative bradycardia"], "a": 0, "sec": ["4.1", "4.3"]}, {"id": "21-8", "ch": 21, "n": 8, "stem": "Congenital Zika syndrome includes:", "opts": ["Patent ductus arteriosus and cataracts as the main features", "Hydrops fetalis due to parvovirus-like anaemia", "Severe microcephaly with subcortical calcifications and eye lesions", "Hutchinson teeth and saddle nose"], "a": 2, "sec": ["4.4"]}, {"id": "21-9", "ch": 21, "n": 9, "stem": "Which patient is at highest risk of death from West Nile neuroinvasive disease?", "opts": ["A 25-year-old healthy woman", "A 10-year-old child with asthma", "A 40-year-old man with treated hypertension", "A 75-year-old man receiving anti-CD20 therapy for lymphoma"], "a": 3, "sec": ["4.5"]}, {"id": "21-10", "ch": 21, "n": 10, "stem": "The Faget sign, typical of yellow fever, is:", "opts": ["Bilateral non-purulent conjunctivitis", "Fever with relative bradycardia", "Petechiae after inflating a blood pressure cuff", "Flaccid paralysis of one limb"], "a": 1, "sec": ["4.6"]}, {"id": "21-11", "ch": 21, "n": 11, "stem": "A traveller returning from Thailand has had fever for 2 days. Which test is most appropriate to diagnose dengue now?", "opts": ["NS1 antigen and/or RT-PCR on blood", "IgG avidity only", "Plaque reduction neutralization test only", "Blood smear for dengue inclusions"], "a": 0, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "21-12", "ch": 21, "n": 12, "stem": "A 70-year-old man in Lazio develops encephalitis in August. Which result best confirms West Nile neuroinvasive disease?", "opts": ["Positive serum IgG", "Positive blood culture", "Detection of WNV-specific IgM in the cerebrospinal fluid", "Raised CSF lactate"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "21-13", "ch": 21, "n": 13, "stem": "A patient previously vaccinated against yellow fever and tick-borne encephalitis has a positive West Nile IgM. What is the main concern?", "opts": ["The result proves acute infection with all three viruses", "IgM never cross-reacts among flaviviruses", "Flavivirus cross-reactivity; the result should be confirmed with a neutralization test", "The patient should receive a new yellow fever vaccine dose"], "a": 2, "sec": ["5.2"]}, {"id": "21-14", "ch": 21, "n": 14, "stem": "In a patient with suspected dengue, the recommended drug for fever and pain is:", "opts": ["Paracetamol", "Ibuprofen", "Aspirin", "Ketorolac"], "a": 0, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "21-15", "ch": 21, "n": 15, "stem": "According to current guidelines, which intervention is NOT recommended in severe dengue?", "opts": ["Crystalloid fluids", "Capillary refill time to guide fluid therapy", "Lactate measurement", "Prophylactic platelet transfusion in a patient without bleeding"], "a": 3, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "21-16", "ch": 21, "n": 16, "stem": "In a patient with liver failure due to yellow fever, current guidelines suggest:", "opts": ["High-dose paracetamol", "Intravenous N-acetylcysteine", "Systemic corticosteroids", "Intravenous immunoglobulin"], "a": 1, "sec": ["6.1"]}, {"id": "21-17", "ch": 21, "n": 17, "stem": "In September, a woman returns to Bologna from Cuba with fever and rash, and dengue is suspected. Besides notification, which measure is most important to prevent local transmission?", "opts": ["Isolation in a negative-pressure room", "Protection against mosquito bites while viremic, plus vector control around her home", "Antibiotic prophylaxis for household contacts", "Immediate vaccination of all her contacts"], "a": 1, "sec": ["5.5", "7.5"]}, {"id": "21-18", "ch": 21, "n": 18, "stem": "Which statement about the yellow fever vaccine is correct?", "opts": ["It is an inactivated vaccine that is safe in all immunocompromised patients", "It must be repeated every 10 years for all travellers", "It is recommended for infants aged 3 months", "A single dose gives lifelong protection for most people"], "a": 3, "sec": ["8.3"]}, {"id": "21-19", "ch": 21, "n": 19, "stem": "Why is the CYD-TDV (Dengvaxia) vaccine given only to people with previous dengue infection?", "opts": ["In seronegative people it increases the risk of severe dengue at a later natural infection", "It is ineffective in people who have had dengue", "It contains a bacterial adjuvant that is dangerous in seronegative people", "It causes chikungunya in seronegative people"], "a": 0, "sec": ["8.3"]}, {"id": "21-20", "ch": 21, "n": 20, "stem": "Which chikungunya vaccine is a non-live virus-like particle vaccine?", "opts": ["Ixchiq (VLA1553)", "Qdenga (TAK-003)", "Vimkunya", "Ixiaro"], "a": 2, "sec": ["8.3"]}];
